Since 28 February 2026, the [[United States]] and  [[Israel]] have been at war with [[Iran]] and [[Axis of Resistance|its regional allies]]. Hostilities broke out after US–Israeli airstrikes killed [[List of Iranian officials killed during the 2026 Iran war|several Iranian officials]], including Supreme Leader [[assassination of Ali Khamenei|Ali Khamenei]]. The strikes were launched amid ongoing [[2025–2026 Iran–United States negotiations|US–Iran negotiations]] regarding [[Nuclear program of Iran|Iran's nuclear program]]. 

The war is part of [[Middle Eastern crisis (2023–present)|a Middle Eastern crisis]] sparked by the [[Gaza war]]. It follows the [[Twelve-Day War]] and was preceded by the largest [[2026 United States military buildup in the Middle East|US military buildup]] in the region since the [[2003 invasion of Iraq]]. US [[Rationale for the 2026 Iran war|justifications for initiating the war]] included a response to the [[2025–2026 Iran massacres|crackdown]] on [[2025–2026 Iranian protests|anti-government protests]] in Iran, preventing Iran from obtaining a nuclear weapon, [[Regime change efforts in the 2026 Iran war|regime change]], and taking [[Oil reserves in Iran|Iran's oil]] and [[Natural gas in Iran|gas resources]]. [[Reactions to the 2026 Iran war|Critics]] have rejected these justifications, maintaining that the attack initiated a [[war of aggression]], in violation of [[international law]].

Following [[Mosaic defence strategy|a decentralized strategy]], Iranian units responded with a series of missile and drone attacks against Israel, [[Arab League–Iran relations|US-aligned Arab countries]], and [[United States foreign policy in the Middle East|US military bases across the region]], and by [[2026 Strait of Hormuz crisis|blocking the Strait of Hormuz]] in late March. Following the strikes on their territory, the [[United Arab Emirates]], [[Saudi Arabia]], and [[Kuwait]] also attacked Iran and skirmished with [[Iranian intervention in Iraq (2014–present)|its Iraqi non-state allies]].  Two days after Khamenei's assassination, [[2026 Lebanon war|fighting resumed]] between [[Hezbollah]] and Israel. Israel began [[Israeli occupation of Southern Lebanon (2026)|occupying southern Lebanon]] in March. The war has resulted in [[Casualties of the 2026 Iran war|thousands of civilian and military casualties]]. The [[Economic impact of the 2026 Iran war|economic impact of the war]] included the largest ever [[2026 Iran war fuel crisis|supply disruption of the global oil market]], disruptions of the natural gas, fertilizer, aviation, and tourism industries, and volatility in financial markets.<!-- Do not remove US military financial cost from the lead without a talk consensus --> {{As of|2026|06|df=dm}}, the cost of the war to the US was estimated at $113.3 billion,<ref> {{#invoke:cite |web |url=https://iran-cost-ticker.com/ |title=Iran War Cost Tracker — Final Total: $113.3 Billion |date=16 June 2026 |language=en |access-date=3 July 2026 |work=Iran War Cost Tracker}}</ref><ref name="AJCost">{{#invoke:cite |news |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/4/30/25bn-or-1-trillion-how-much-has-iran-war-really-cost-the-us |title=$25bn or $1 Trillion: How Much Has Iran War Really Cost the US? |date=30 March 2026 |language=en |access-date=24 May 2026 |work=[[Al Jazeera English]]}}</ref> while the Trump administration asked for $1.5 trillion for [[United States Department of Defense|the Pentagon]] over the next fiscal year, setting a record in modern US military spending.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c70gek2kqyno|title=Iran war has cost US $37.5bn so far, Hegseth says, as Pentagon seeks billions more|website=BBC News|date=22 July 2026}}</ref>

The [[Secretary-General of the United Nations|secretary-general of the United Nations]] and several countries condemned the US–Israeli strikes, while the [[United Nations Security Council]] passed [[United Nations Security Council Resolution 2817|a resolution]] condemning Iran's retaliatory strikes on Gulf states. A [[2026 Iran war ceasefire|two-week ceasefire]] beginning on 8 April came under strain after Iran responded to [[8 April 2026 Israeli attacks on Lebanon|Israeli attacks on Lebanon]] by resuming its blockade.<ref>{{#invoke:Cite |web |last1=Hancock |first1=Alice |last2=John |first2=Jamie |last3=Harris |first3=Lee |date=8 April 2026 |title=Shipping Stalls as Tehran Dictates Terms in Strait of Hormuz |url=https://www.ft.com/content/0ce6a1a0-08b0-4b2b-9634-e5c03c4fbbcd?syn-25a6b1a6=1 |website=Financial Times}}</ref> After the failure of the [[Islamabad Talks]] on 12–13 April, the US imposed [[2026 United States naval blockade of Iran|its own naval blockade]]. A [[Islamabad Memorandum|memorandum to end the war]] was signed on 17 June by the presidents of the US and Iran, lifting the dual blockade. Tensions again escalated, but on 28 June, the US and Iran agreed to cease their exchange of attacks. On 8 July, the ceasefire deal between the US and Iran collapsed after attacks by both sides.
