== Names ==
The initial strikes on Iran were [[Code name|codenamed]] Operation Epic Fury by the [[Second presidency of Donald Trump|United States government]],<ref name="ii-epicfury"/><ref name="FT01"/><ref>{{Cite web |date=21 April 2026 |title=Operation Epic Fury and International Law |url=https://www.state.gov/releases/office-of-the-legal-adviser/2026/04/operation-epic-fury-and-international-law/ |access-date=8 June 2026 |publisher=United States Department of State |language=en-US}}</ref> and Operation Roaring Lion{{efn|{{langx|he|מִבְצַע שְׁאָגַת הָאֲרִי|Mivtsá She'agát Ha'arí|engvar=ox}}, {{IPA|he|mivˈt͡sä ʃe̞.äˈɡätʰ hä.ʔäˈʁi|pron|small=yes}}}} by the [[Thirty-seventh government of Israel|Israeli government]],<ref name="cnn-roaringlion"/><ref name="ynet-lion"/> in line with their codename of Operation Rising Lion for their strikes on Iran during the [[Twelve-Day War]] in June 2025. Iran codenamed its response {{vanchor|Operation True Promise IV}}.{{efn|Previously, Iran had launched [[Operation True Promise I|I]], [[Operation True Promise II|II]], and [[Operation True Promise III|III]] on a more limited scale.}}{{efn|{{langx|fa|عملیات وعده صادق ۴|Amaliyat-e Va'deh-ye Sadegh 4}}}}

The war has also been referred to by some outlets as the "Third Gulf War" (Gulf War III), following the [[Gulf War]] (1990–1991), and the [[Iraq War]] (2003–2011), which has also been referred to as the "Second Gulf War" (Gulf War II).{{efn|Multiple sources: <ref>{{cite news |title=The third Gulf war will scar energy markets for a long time yet |url=https://www.economist.com/finance-and-economics/2026/04/08/the-third-gulf-war-will-scar-energy-markets-for-a-long-time-yet |newspaper=[[The Economist]] |date=8 April 2026 |access-date=28 July 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Hopton |first=Nicholas |title=What next in the Third Gulf War? |url=https://www.newstatesman.com/international-politics/2026/03/what-next-in-the-third-gulf-war |work=[[New Statesman]] |date=23 March 2026 |access-date=28 July 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Stein |first1=Aaron |last2=Salisbury |first2=Emma |last3=Lair |first3=Sam |last4=Petersen |first4=Michael B. |last5=Plopsky |first5=Guy |title=Understanding the Third Gulf War |url=https://www.fpri.org/article/2026/03/fpri-experts-react-understanding-the-third-gulf-war/ |work=[[Foreign Policy Research Institute]] |date=5 March 2026 |access-date=28 July 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Kacowicz |first=Arie M. |title=When and how will the third Gulf War end? |url=https://blogs.timesofisrael.com/when-and-how-will-the-third-gulf-war-end/ |work=[[The Times of Israel]] |date=11 March 2026 |access-date=28 July 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Hopper |first=Timothy |title=The "Third Gulf War" and the collapse of the oil-for-security bargain |url=https://www.middleeastmonitor.com/20260416-the-third-gulf-war-and-the-collapse-of-the-oil-for-security-bargain/ |work=[[Middle East Monitor]] |date=16 April 2026 |access-date=28 July 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Shelter-Jones |first1=Phillip |title=Gulf War III Is a Warning About the Effects of a 'Taiwan Straits War I' |url=https://www.rusi.org/explore-our-research/publications/commentary/gulf-war-iii-warning-about-effects-taiwan-straits-war-i |website=[[Royal United Services Institute]] |date=11 March 2026 |access-date=28 July 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Wintour |first=Patrick |title=From Bush Sr to Trump: the risks, lessons and legacy of US interference in the Middle East |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/ng-interactive/2026/mar/08/us-interference-middle-east-iraq-iran-conflict-legacy-lessons |date=8 March 2026 |access-date=28 July 2026 |work=[[The Guardian]] |quote=This is the third Gulf war{{nbsp}}...}}</ref>}} Iranian state media has referred to the conflict as the "Third Imposed War",<ref>{{Cite news |date=16 June 2026 |title=Advanced Arms Production Never Halted During Imposed War |url=https://en.mehrnews.com/news/245391/Advanced-arms-production-never-halted-during-imposed-war |access-date=30 June 2026 |work=[[Mehr News Agency]] |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.meforum.org/mef-observer/iranian-media-reinforce-narrative-of-mojtaba-khameneis-leadership |title=Iranian Media Reinforce Narrative of Mojtaba Khamenei's Leadership |last=Soghom |first=Mardo |website=[[Middle East Forum]] |date=10 May 2026 |access-date=30 July 2026 |quote=... following what Iranian media call the 'Third Imposed War.'}}</ref> with the [[Iran–Iraq War]] (1980–1988) and the Twelve-Day War respectively as the "First" and "Second".<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://en.hawzahnews.com/news/377104/Iran-s-Third-Imposed-War-Elevates-Nation-s-Status-as-Civilization-Buildin |title=Iran's 'Third Imposed War' Elevates Nation's Status as Civilization-Building Power, Says Scholar |work=Hawzah News Agency |date=28 April 2026 |access-date=30 July 2026 |quote=The commander's reference to a 'Third Imposed War' follows the official Iranian narrative that frames the 1980-88 Iran-Iraq conflict as the first imposed war}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |url=https://wanaen.com/the-end-of-the-weak-iran-narrative/ |title=The End of the "Weak Iran" Narrative |work=WANA |date=20 March 2026 |access-date=30 July 2026 |quote=... from the aftermath of the 'Second Imposed War' (the Twelve-Day War) to the beginning of the 'Third Imposed War' (Iran's current war with the U.S. and Israel{{nbsp}}...)}}</ref> Due to the war starting during the month of [[Ramadan]], it has sometimes been called the "Ramadan War".{{efn|{{langx|fa|جنگ رمضان|Jang-e Ramazān}}}}{{efn|Multiple sources: <ref>{{Cite news |date=7 June 2026 |title=From Online Class to Revolution Square: A Tehran Teacher's Routine Amid War |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2026/6/7/from-online-class-to-revolution-square-a-tehran-teachers-routine-amid-war |access-date=8 June 2026 |agency=[[Al Jazeera English]] |last=Alhaei |first=Rasol |language=en |quote=The 'Ramadan War', as the US-Israel war on Iran is popularly known |archive-date=8 June 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260608143908/https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2026/6/7/from-online-class-to-revolution-square-a-tehran-teachers-routine-amid-war |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=22 May 2026 |title=Intact but Unreformed: Iran After the Ramadan War |url=https://www.thegeopoliticaldesk.com/intact-but-unreformed-iran-after-the-ramadan-war/ |access-date=8 June 2026 |website=The Geopolitical Desk |language=en |url-access=registration |archive-date=8 June 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260608143907/https://www.thegeopoliticaldesk.com/intact-but-unreformed-iran-after-the-ramadan-war/ |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="arabnews-eid"/><ref>{{Cite web |date=13 May 2026 |title=Ramadan War Inflicts $160 Million Blow to Iran's Tourism Sector |url=https://www.tehrantimes.com/news/526400/Ramadan-War-inflicts-160-Million-blow-to-Iran-s-tourism-sector |access-date=8 June 2026 |website=[[Tehran Times]] |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=26 May 2026 |title=Iranian Defense: Development of New Technologies Enabled Us to Target 210 Enemy Aircraft During Ramadan War |url=https://saba.ye/en/news3713128.htm |access-date=8 June 2026 |agency=[[Saba News Agency]] |language=en |archive-date=8 June 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260608145325/https://saba.ye/en/news3713128.htm |url-status=live}}</ref>}}

