=== Iran's relations with the United States and Israel ===
{{Further|Iran–United States relations|Iran–Israel relations}}
<!-- If there are verification issues in this section, it may be due to the removal of references in this diff: https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=2026_Iran_war&diff=1348508231&oldid=1348508155 -->

A US- and UK-backed [[1953 Iranian coup d'état|coup d'état in 1953]] deposed the democratically-elected Iranian prime minister [[Mohammad Mosaddegh]] due to his [[Nationalization of the Iranian oil industry|nationalization of the oil industry]], strengthening the rule of [[Shah]] [[Mohammad Reza Pahlavi]]. Israel maintained ties with Iran as part of its [[alliance of the periphery]] strategy. Resentment of the Shah's autocratic rule led to the [[Iranian Revolution|1979 revolution]] in which Pahlavi was overthrown and replaced by an [[Islamic republic]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Hourcade |first=Bernard |date=March 2026 |title=What Prospects for a New Iran? |url=https://mondediplo.com/2026/03/04iran |access-date=15 April 2026 |website=Le Monde diplomatique |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=Afary |first=Janet |date=13 April 2026 |title=Iranian Revolution |url=https://www.britannica.com/event/Iranian-Revolution |website=Encyclopedia Britannica |access-date=2 May 2026 |archive-date=15 December 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251215031938/https://www.britannica.com/event/Iranian-Revolution |url-status=live}}</ref> Iran severed diplomatic ties with the US and Israel and held the [[Iran hostage crisis|American embassy staff hostage]],<ref name="News1828">{{#invoke:cite |news |date=28 February 2026 |title=From 1953 Coup To 2026 Strikes: The Long Road To US-Iran War |url=https://www.news18.com/world/from-1953-coup-to-2026-strikes-the-long-road-to-us-iran-war-ws-l-9935056.html |access-date=4 March 2026 |work=[[News18]]}}</ref> releasing them after signing the [[Algiers Accords (1981)]], in which, among other things, the US agreed not to intervene in Iranian affairs anymore.<ref>{{cite web |title=Iran Sues U.S. in Hague Tribunal Despite "Near-Zero" Chance of Success |url=https://iranwire.com/en/features/152339-iran-sues-us-in-hague-tribunal-despite-near-zero-chance-of-success/ |website=iranwire.com |language=en}}</ref>

During the [[Iran–Iraq War]], the [[United States support for Iraq during the Iran–Iraq War|US supported Iraq]]. In 1988, [[USS Samuel B. Roberts (FFG-58)#1988 deployment and mine strike|a US warship struck an Iranian mine]], and the US responded by [[Operation Praying Mantis|attacking Iran's navy]]. A few months later, the US mistakenly shot down a [[Iran Air Flight 655|civilian Iranian flight]].<ref name="Al-MarashiCon">{{#invoke:cite |web |last=Al-Marashi |first=Ibrahim |date=2 March 2026 |title=Iran and the US Have Been at War for Decades – and There's No End in Sight |url=https://theconversation.com/iran-and-the-us-have-been-at-war-for-decades-and-theres-no-end-in-sight-277284 |access-date=4 March 2026 |website=[[The Conversation (website)|The Conversation]] |language=en}}</ref><ref name="MacFarquhar1926">{{#invoke:cite |news |last1=MacFarquhar |first1=Neil |title=A Timeline of the Tensions Between the Islamic Republic of Iran and the U.S. |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/15/world/middleeast/united-states-iran-history.html |access-date=19 March 2026 |work=[[The New York Times]] |date=15 March 2026}}</ref> Iran started [[Ballistic missile program of Iran|a ballistic missile program]] to deter Iraqi [[War of the Cities|missile attacks on Iranian cities]] during the 1980–88 Iran–Iraq War and to compensate for its lack of a modern air force due to sanctions.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |title=Iran Unveils New Solid-Fueled Ballistic Missile, State TV Reports |url=https://apnews.com/article/iran-us-israel-ballistic-missile-dd4500eaf8553f6375eab10a884dabd2 |access-date=28 March 2026 |work=Associated Press |date=4 May 2025 |language=en}}</ref>

Starting in the 1985, Iran began engaging in [[Proxy war|proxy conflicts]] throughout the Middle East.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.upi.com/Archives/1985/02/16/Israel-leaves-front-lines-in-south-Lebanon/4591477378000/|title=Israel leaves front lines in south Lebanon|work=UPI|access-date=7 July 2026|archive-date=23 October 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201023043753/https://www.upi.com/Archives/1985/02/16/Israel-leaves-front-lines-in-south-Lebanon/4591477378000/|url-status=live}}</ref> These proxy groups became part of the informal '[[Axis of Resistance]]' committed primarily to countering [[Iran–United States relations|American]], [[Iran–Israel proxy conflict|Israeli]], [[Iran–Saudi Arabia proxy war|Saudi]], Emirati, and sometimes [[Iran–Turkey proxy conflict|Turkish]] influence in the region.<ref name="Hubbard-2024">{{Cite news |last1=Hubbard |first1=Ben |last2=Rubin |first2=Alissa J. |date=30 September 2024 |title=Facing a Big Test, Iran's 'Axis of Resistance' Flails |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2024/09/30/world/middleeast/iran-axis-of-resistance.html |work=The New York Times |archive-date=9 October 2024 |access-date=7 July 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241009133520/https://www.nytimes.com/2024/09/30/world/middleeast/iran-axis-of-resistance.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="AlKassab-2023">{{Cite news |last=Al-Kassab |first=Fatima |date=26 October 2023 |title=What is the 'axis of resistance' of Iran-backed groups in the Middle East? |url=https://www.npr.org/2023/10/26/1208456496/iran-hamas-axis-of-resistance-hezbollah-israel |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231029183838/https://www.npr.org/2023/10/26/1208456496/iran-hamas-axis-of-resistance-hezbollah-israel |archive-date=29 October 2023 |access-date=7 July 2026 |work=[[NPR]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=11 June 2025 |title=The shape-shifting 'axis of resistance' |url=https://www.chathamhouse.org/2025/03/shape-shifting-axis-resistance |access-date=7 July 2026 |website=Chatham House |language=en |archive-date=24 February 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260224200319/https://www.chathamhouse.org/2025/03/shape-shifting-axis-resistance |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=18 February 2026 |title=The Evolving Middle Eastern Regional Order: Türkiye-Iraq Relations in Context |url=https://carnegieendowment.org/russia-eurasia/research/2026/02/the-evolving-middle-eastern-regional-order-turkiye-iraq-relations-in-context |access-date=7 July 2026 |website=Carnegie Endowment for International Peace |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Ali-Khan |first=Veena |date=19 March 2025 |title=Down but Not Out: Reassessing the Axis of Resistance |url=https://tcf.org/content/report/down-but-not-out-reassessing-the-axis-of-resistance/ |access-date=7 July 2026 |website=The Century Foundation |language=en}}</ref>

In 2002, Iran's covert [[nuclear program of Iran|nuclear program]] was first revealed, and Iran denied seeking a weapon. In 2003, Iran's [[AMAD Project]] was suspended following Supreme Leader [[Ali Khamenei's fatwa against nuclear weapons]].<ref>{{cite book |last1=Khalaji |first1=Mehdi |last2=Collier |first2=Robert |chapter-url=https://www.washingtoninstitute.org/uploads/Documents/pubs/PolicyFocus115.pdf |chapter=2. Shiite Jurisprudence, Political Expediency, and Nuclear Weapons |title=Nuclear Fatwa; Religion and Politics in Iran's Proliferation Strategy |pages=13–14, 25 |publisher=[[Washington Institute for Near East Policy]] |date=September 2011 |number=15 |archive-date=4 June 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190604101105/https://www.washingtoninstitute.org/uploads/Documents/pubs/PolicyFocus115.pdf |quote=Khamenei's Nuclear [[Fatwa]] Khamenei's statements on the religious prohibition against the production and use of WMD "in any form" were apparently first recorded in October 2003}}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |first=David |last=Gritten |date=18 June 2025 |title=Was Iran Months Away from Producing a Nuclear Bomb? |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cn840275p5yo |access-date=2 March 2026 |work=[[BBC News]] |language=en-GB |quote=the US intelligence community "continues to assess that Iran is not building a nuclear weapon and Supreme Leader [Ayatollah Ali] Khamenei has not authorised the nuclear weapons programme that he suspended in 2003". – What do we know about Iran's nuclear programme? a decade-long investigation by the IAEA found evidence that Iran conducted "a range of activities relevant to the development of a nuclear explosive device" from the late 1980s until 2003}}</ref> In 2005, the US began imposing [[United States sanctions against Iran|sanctions targeting Iran's nuclear program]], and in 2006 the [[United Nations Security Council]] (UNSC) imposed [[International sanctions against Iran#UN sanctions against Iran|a series of sanctions against Iran]]. The US and Israel conducted [[Operation Olympic Games|a campaign of cyberwarfare]] against Iranian nuclear facilities to disrupt their operations, while Israel [[Assassinations of Iranian nuclear scientists|assassinated several top Iranian nuclear scientists]].<ref name="MacFarquhar1926"/><ref name="Le Monde Assassinations">{{cite web |last1=Imbert |last2=Golshiri |last3=Mas |first1=Louis |first2=Ghazal |first3=Liselotte |title=How Israel Tracked Down and Assassinated Scientists Involved in Iran's Nuclear Program |url=https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2025/07/08/how-israel-tracked-down-and-assassinated-scientists-involved-in-iran-s-nuclear-program_6743166_4.html |website=lemonde.fr |publisher=Le Monde |access-date=21 April 2026 |date=8 July 2025 |archive-date=2 May 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260502153905/https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2025/07/08/how-israel-tracked-down-and-assassinated-scientists-involved-in-iran-s-nuclear-program_6743166_4.html |url-status=live}}</ref> The UNSC concerns about Iran's nuclear program from 2006{{efn|The problems indicated by the UNSC during 2006 in their first [[Resolution (law)|resolution]] were specifically "[[uranium enrichment]]<ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |author=[[UN Security Council]] |date=31 July 2006 |url=https://main.un.org/securitycouncil/en/s/res/1696-%282006%29 |title=S/Res/1696 (2006) |website=[[United Nations]] |quote=Expresses concern at the intentions of Iran's nuclear programme and demands that Iran halt its uranium enrichment programme}}</ref> and reprocessing"<ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |author=[[UN Security Council]] |url=https://docs.un.org/en/s/res/1696%20(2006) |title=Resolution 1696 (2006) |page=2 |website=[[United Nations]] |quote=2. ''Demands'', in this context, that Iran shall suspend all enrichment-related and reprocessing activities, including research and development, to be verified by the IAEA}}</ref> in the second resolution of that year; additionally use or attempted use of "[[heavy water]]".<ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |author=[[UN Security Council]] |url=https://docs.un.org/en/s/res/1737%20(2006) |title=Resolution 1737 (2006) |page=2 |website=[[United Nations]] |quote=2. ''Decides'', in this context, that Iran shall without further delay suspend the following proliferation sensitive nuclear activities: (a) all enrichment-related and reprocessing activities, including research and development, to be verified by the IAEA; and (b) work on all heavy water-related projects, including the construction of a research reactor moderated by heavy water, also to be verified by the IAEA;}}</ref>}} led to the multilateral [[Joint Comprehensive Plan of Action]] (JCPOA) between Iran and the [[P5+1]] and the EU in 2015.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |author=[[UN Security Council]] |url=https://main.un.org/securitycouncil/en/content/2231/background?_gl=1*9yohww*_ga*ODAzNjU1MjU1LjE3NzI2NDk4NTI.*_ga_S5EKZKSB78*czE3NzI2ODc0NjckbzIkZzAkdDE3NzI2ODc0NzAkajU3JGwwJGgw*_ga_TK9BQL5X7Z*czE3NzI2ODc0NjckbzIkZzAkdDE3NzI2ODc0NjckajYwJGwwJGgw |title=Resolution 2231 (2015) on Iran Nuclear Issue |website=[[United Nations]] |quote=resolutions 1696 (2006), 1737 (2006), 1747 (2007), 1803 (2008), 1835 (2008) and 1929 (2010) – Background Diplomatic efforts to reach a comprehensive, long-term and proper solution to the Iranian nuclear issue culminated in the Joint Comprehensive Plan of Action (JCPOA) concluded on 14 July 2015}}</ref>

In January 2020, US president [[Donald Trump]] ordered the [[assassination of Qasem Soleimani]], the commander of the [[Quds Force|Iranian Quds Force]].<ref name="News1828"/><ref name="Al-MarashiCon"/> Following the [[October 7 attacks]] on Israel and the start of the [[Gaza war]], tensions further escalated with Israel fighting Iran-backed militias across the Middle East, including [[Hamas]],{{efn|Sometimes included in the "[[Axis of Resistance]]".<ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |date=11 July 2018 |title=Flexible Resistance: How Hezbollah and Hamas Are Mending Ties |url=https://carnegieendowment.org/research/2018/07/flexible-resistance-how-hezbollah-and-hamas-are-mending-ties?lang=en&center=middle-east |access-date=8 February 2026 |website=[[Carnegie Endowment for International Peace]] |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |date=10 October 2023 |title=What Links Hamas to the 'Axis of Resistance' and Iran as Its Patron? |url=https://www.irishtimes.com/world/middle-east/2023/10/10/what-links-hamas-to-the-axis-of-resistance-and-iran-as-its-patron/ |access-date=8 February 2026 |newspaper=[[The Irish Times]] |language=en}}</ref>}} [[Hezbollah–Israel conflict (2023–present)|Hezbollah]], and the [[Red Sea crisis|Houthis]]. [[Israeli airstrike on the Iranian consulate in Damascus|Israeli strikes on the Iranian consulate in Damascus]] and the [[Targeted killing by Israel|assassinations]] of [[Assassination of Ismail Haniyeh|Ismail Haniyeh]] and [[2024 Hezbollah headquarters strike|Hassan Nasrallah]] in 2024 were met with Iranian strikes on Israel in [[April 2024 Iranian strikes on Israel|April]] and [[October 2024 Iranian strikes on Israel|October]].<ref>{{#invoke:Cite|news|title=Evolution Under Fire: Iran's 'Axis of Resistance' in a Post-War Era|url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/6/30/evolution-under-fire-irans-axis-of-resistance-in-a-post-war-era|date=30 June 2026|work=Al Jazeera English}}</ref> In June 2025, Israel launched the [[Twelve-Day War]] by attacking Iranian military and nuclear facilities, provoking Iranian counter-strikes. The United States also [[2025 United States strikes on Iranian nuclear sites|struck Iranian nuclear facilities]] during the Twelve-Day War, which ended in [[Twelve-Day War ceasefire|a ceasefire]].<ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |date= |title=Iran's Conflict With Israel and the United States |url=https://www.cfr.org/global-conflict-tracker/conflict/confrontation-between-united-states-and-iran |website=[[Council on Foreign Relations]]}}</ref>

In early 2026, Israeli prime minister [[Benjamin Netanyahu]] lobbied President Donald Trump for a joint military strike on Iran, specifically targeting its leadership.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Swan |first1=Jonathan |last2=Haberman |first2=Maggie |title=How Trump Took the U.S. To War With Iran |work=[[The New York Times]] |date=7 April 2026 |issn=0362-4331 |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/07/us/politics/trump-iran-war.html |language=en-US |page=}}</ref> Following high-level meetings in February, Trump authorized "Operation Epic Fury", with reports citing [[Israeli intelligence community|Israeli intelligence]] provided by Netanyahu as a decisive factor in the decision.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Banco |first1=Erin |last2=Slattery |first2=Gram |last3=Lubell |first3=Maayan |title=Exclusive: Trump Approved Iran Operation After Netanyahu Argued for Joint Killing of Khamenei, Sources Say |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/middle-east/trump-approved-iran-operation-after-netanyahu-argued-joint-killing-khamenei-2026-03-23/ |work=[[Reuters]] |date=24 March 2026}}</ref>

===Nuclear activity in the Middle East===

==== Iranian nuclear program ====
{{Main|Nuclear program of Iran}}
{{See also|Treaty on the Non-Proliferation of Nuclear Weapons#Iran|Iran and weapons of mass destruction#Nuclear weapons}}

Nuclear enrichment has been considered a component of Iranian national development plans since the Shah's regime, when the first nuclear program was developed.<ref>{{Cite web |date=1 July 2026 |title=The History of Iran's Nuclear Program—With Sina Azodi |url=https://www.thenation.com/podcast/politics/amprest-070126/ |access-date=26 July 2026 |website=The Nation |language=en-US}}</ref> Iran retained its nuclear program after the 1979 revolution, and has long denied seeking nuclear weapons, saying its enrichment efforts were to generate [[nuclear power]] for civilian use.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |date=24 July 2025 |title=Iran Reaffirms Right to Enrich Uranium Ahead of Key Talks in Turkiye |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2025/7/24/iran-reaffirms-right-to-enrich-uranium-ahead-of-key-talks-in-turkiye |access-date=5 March 2026 |agency=[[Al Jazeera English]] |language=en |quote=Western powers, led by the US and backed by Israel, have long accused Tehran of secretly seeking nuclear weapons. Iran has repeatedly denied this, insisting its nuclear programme is solely for civilian purposes such as energy production.}}</ref> Despite Iran's compliance with the JCPOA, Trump [[United States withdrawal from the JCPOA|withdrew the US]] from the deal in 2018,<ref>{{cite web |date=8 June 2018 |title=IAEA Report Confirms Iran's Compliance with the JCPOA |url=https://www.armscontrol.org/blog/2018-06-08/iaea-report-confirms-irans-compliance-jcpoa |work=[[Arms Control Association]] |access-date=14 April 2026 |archive-date=20 April 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260420100238/https://www.armscontrol.org/blog/2018-06-08/iaea-report-confirms-irans-compliance-jcpoa |url-status=live}}</ref>  re-imposed [[United States sanctions against Iran|sanctions]], and adopted a "[[Maximum pressure campaign|maximum pressure]]" strategy.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |date=29 June 2018 |title=Trump Tightens the Screws on Iran's Oil |url=https://www.brookings.edu/articles/trump-tightens-the-screws-on-irans-oil/ |access-date=28 February 2026 |publisher=[[Brookings Institution]] |language=en-US}}</ref> The [[Biden administration]] kept most of these sanctions, further damaging Iran's economy.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |last=Shamim |first=Sarah |date=13 January 2026 |title=How US Sanctions Crippled Lives of Iranians Trump Says He Wants to 'Help' |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/1/13/how-us-sanctions-crippled-lives-of-iranians-trump-says-he-wants-to-help |access-date=4 March 2026 |agency=[[Al Jazeera English]] |language=en}}</ref> Trump continued the "maximum pressure" approach in his second term.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |date=4 February 2025 |title=Trump Reimposes 'Maximum Pressure' on Iran, Aims to Drive Oil Exports to Zero |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/us/trump-set-reimpose-maximum-pressure-iran-official-says-2025-02-04/ |access-date=3 March 2026 |agency=[[Reuters]] |language=en-US}}</ref> In September 2025, the UN reimposed [[International sanctions against Iran|sanctions on Iran]] using the [[Snapback mechanism of sanctions against Iran|"snapback" mechanism]], causing the [[Iranian rial|currency]] to weaken, resulting in price increases for food supplies such as meat, rice, and other staples, freezing Iranian assets abroad, suspending arms transactions, and imposing penalties related to the country's [[Ballistic missile program of Iran|ballistic-missile programme]].<ref>{{Cite news |date=29 December 2025 |title=Protests Erupt in Iran over Currency's Plunge to Record Low |url=https://apnews.com/article/iran-traders-protest-rial-currency-ddc955739fb412b642251dee10638f03 |access-date=23 April 2025 |work=[[Associated Press News]] |archive-date=30 December 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251230042106/https://apnews.com/article/iran-traders-protest-rial-currency-ddc955739fb412b642251dee10638f03 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:Cite|news |title=European Powers Warn Iran Against 'Escalatory' Acts As Snapback Sanctions Take Force |url=https://www.rferl.org/a/iran-snapback-sanctions-european-e3-un-nuclear/33543002.html |date=28 September 2025 |agency=[[Radio Free Europe/Radio Liberty]]}}</ref> [[Secretary of the Treasury|Treasury Secretary]] [[Scott Bessent]] labelled the collapse of the Iranian currency in December 2025 as the strategy's "grand culmination".<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |last=Sharma |first=Yashraj |date=3 February 2026 |title=US Says It Caused Dollar Shortage to Trigger Iran Protests: What That Means |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2026/2/13/us-says-it-caused-dollar-shortage-to-trigger-iran-protests-what-that-means |access-date=3 March 2026 |agency=[[Al Jazeera English]] |language=en |quote=In a stunning admission, US Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent said Washington engineered a dollar shortage to send the Iranian rial into freefall that culminated with protesters taking to the streets.}}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |last1=Northam |first1=Jackie |date=23 February 2026 |title=Trump's Sanctions on Iran Have Dramatically Affected Its Economy and Led to Protests |url=https://www.npr.org/2026/02/23/nx-s1-5708935/trumps-sanctions-on-iran-have-dramatically-affected-its-economy-and-led-to-protests |agency=[[NPR]] |access-date=14 March 2026}}</ref> At his [[2026 State of the Union Address|State of the Union Address]] in February 2026, Trump claimed Iran had restarted its nuclear program and was developing missiles capable of striking the US.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |date=25 February 2026 |title=Trump Lays out Case for Possible Attack on Iran in State of the Union Speech |url=https://www.straitstimes.com/world/united-states/update-3-trump-lays-out-case-for-possible-attack-on-iran-in-state-of-the-union?ref=inline-article |access-date=1 March 2026 |work=[[The Straits Times]] |language=en |issn=0585-3923 |quote=He accused Iran of restarting its nuclear programme, working to build missiles that 'soon' would be capable of reaching the US – Iranian state media have claimed that Tehran is developing a missile capable of reaching North America.}}</ref> Trump's claims contradicted his previous claims that Iran's nuclear program was "obliterated" and US intelligence reports that Iran did not pose any threat towards the US, and needed until 2035 to build such missiles to do so.<ref>{{cite news |last=Roth |first=Andrew |date=27 February 2026 |title=Trump Advisers Scramble to Justify Possible US Military Intervention in Iran |work=[[The Guardian]] |url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/feb/27/trump-iran-war-evidence |access-date=17 April 2026}}</ref>

On 12 June 2025, the [[International Atomic Energy Agency]] (IAEA) found Iran [[Background to the Iran–Israel war#Iran nuclear deal, U.S. withdrawal, and non-compliance|non-compliant]] with its NPT safeguards agreement for the first time since 2005.<ref name="Murphy">{{Cite news |last=Murphy |first=Francois |date=12 June 2025 |title=IAEA Board Declares Iran in Breach of Non-Proliferation Obligations |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/china/iaea-board-declares-iran-breach-non-proliferation-duties-diplomats-say-2025-06-12/ |access-date=17 June 2025 |work=[[Reuters]] |language=en |archive-date=22 April 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260422085643/https://www.reuters.com/world/china/iaea-board-declares-iran-breach-non-proliferation-duties-diplomats-say-2025-06-12/ |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="nuclearwatchdog">{{Cite news |last=Bozorgmehr |first=Najmeh |date=25 June 2025 |title=Iran's Parliament Votes to Halt Co-Operation With Nuclear Watchdog |url=https://www.ft.com/content/b822152b-d0a4-4bff-83d5-a79afd88314e |url-access=subscription |access-date=25 June 2025 |work=[[Financial Times]] |archive-date=25 June 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250625114223/https://www.ft.com/content/b822152b-d0a4-4bff-83d5-a79afd88314e |url-status=live}}</ref> A few days later, it was reported that Iran had stored [[highly enriched uranium]] in an underground facility that was undamaged by [[2025 United States strikes on Iranian nuclear sites|US bombings in 2025]].<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |last=Murphy |first=Francois |date=27 February 2026 |title=IAEA Report Says Iran Must Allow Inspections, Points at Isfahan |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/middle-east/iran-stored-highly-enriched-uranium-underground-site-iaea-report-says-2026-02-27/ |access-date=28 February 2026 |agency=[[Reuters]]}}</ref> Subsequently, Iran denied the IAEA access to nuclear facilities that had been bombed, but did provide access to all unaffected facilities.<ref name="AP News IAEA Post War">{{cite news |last1=Liechtenstein |first1=Stephanie |date=27 February 2026 |title=UN Nuclear Watchdog Says It's Unable to Verify Whether Iran Has Suspended All Uranium Enrichment |url=https://apnews.com/article/iran-nuclear-iaea-uranium-enrichment-suspend-ccf574a324504b985f4b158f9d3d6941 |work=[[Associated Press News]] |access-date=16 April 2026 |archive-date=16 April 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260416120655/https://apnews.com/article/iran-nuclear-iaea-uranium-enrichment-suspend-ccf574a324504b985f4b158f9d3d6941 |url-status=live}}</ref> [[Mohammad Eslami]], the head of the [[Atomic Energy Organization of Iran]], said Iran would not permit inspections of facilities struck by the US until the IAEA established rules for the post-war situation, insisting on codified protocols for internationally protected facilities subjected to military attacks, and that the IAEA condemn the attacks.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |date=24 December 2025 |title=Iran Rejects Inspections of Bombed Nuclear Sites Without IAEA Framework |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2025/12/24/iran-rejects-inspections-of-bombed-nuclear-sites-without-iaea-framework |access-date=5 March 2026 |agency=[[Al Jazeera English]] |language=en}}</ref>

In February 2026, Iran informed the IAEA that normal safeguards were "legally untenable and materially impracticable," as a result of threats and "acts of aggression," leaving the IAEA unable to verify whether Iran had suspended enrichment or confirm the status of its stockpile,<ref name="AP News IAEA Post War" /> though it found no evidence Iran was weaponizing.<ref>{{cite web |date=4 March 2026 |title=IAEA Says No Evidence Iran Is Building a Nuclear Bomb |url=https://www.middleeastmonitor.com/20260304-iaea-says-no-evidence-iran-is-building-a-nuclear-bomb/ |website=middleeastmonitor.com |publisher=Middle East Monitor |access-date=16 April 2026 |archive-date=19 April 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260419055035/https://www.middleeastmonitor.com/20260304-iaea-says-no-evidence-iran-is-building-a-nuclear-bomb/ |url-status=live}}</ref> Analysts in the UK and US characterized Iran's strategy as [[Nuclear latency|nuclear hedging]].<ref>{{bulleted list|
| {{cite journal |last1=Bowen |first1=Wyn |last2=Moran |first2=Matthew |location=[[King's College London]] |date=7 March 2014 |url=https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/13523260.2014.884338 |title=Iran's Nuclear Programme: A Case Study in Hedging? |journal=Contemporary Security Policy |volume=35 |issue=1 |pages=26–52 |doi=10.1080/13523260.2014.884338 |publisher=[[Taylor & Francis]] |issn=1743-8764 |quote=this assessment is further complicated by Iran's domestic political context, which has engendered an approach that is as much 'hedging by default' as it is 'hedging by design'. This approach allows Tehran to reconcile restraint with domestic consensus on nuclear advancement.}}
| {{cite journal |last1=Bowen |first1=Wyn |last2=Moran |first2=Matthew |year=2015 |url=https://www.chathamhouse.org/sites/default/files/field/field_document/INTA91_4_01_BowenMoran.pdf |title=Living with nuclear hedging: the implications of Iran's nuclear strategy |journal=[[International Relations (journal)|International Affairs]] |volume=91 |issue=4 |page=688-paragraph 2 |publisher=[[The Royal Institute of International Affairs]]: [[John Wiley & Sons]] |doi=10.1111/1468-2346.12337 |quote=There is much evidence to suggest Iran is engaged in a more nuanced strategy based on nuclear hedging, rather than outright pursuit of the bomb. |archive-date=2 March 2026 |access-date=6 March 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260302202048/https://www.chathamhouse.org/sites/default/files/field/field_document/INTA91_4_01_BowenMoran.pdf |url-status=live}}
| {{#invoke:cite |web |last=Eisenstadt |first=Michael |date=29 November 2022 |url=https://www.washingtoninstitute.org/policy-analysis/irans-nuclear-hedging-strategy-shaping-islamic-republics-proliferation-calculus |title=Iran's Nuclear Hedging Strategy: Shaping the Islamic Republic's Proliferation Calculus |publisher=[[The Washington Institute for Near East Policy]]}}
| {{cite journal |last=Gettel |date=December 2024 |first=Christopher |location=George Mason University |url=https://doaj.org/article/852466ce63b74e73a6608e21d8369846 |title=Why Iran is Choosing Nuclear Hedging |journal=Panoply Journal |volume=5 |publisher=Center for International Relations and International Security Inc |doi=10.71166/2x5v5824 |issn=2766-2594 |doi-access=free |archive-date=19 April 2026 |access-date=6 March 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260419232505/https://doaj.org/article/852466ce63b74e73a6608e21d8369846 |url-status=live}}
| {{cite book |last=Volpe |first=Tristan |location=[[Naval Postgraduate School]] |date=23 February 2023 |chapter-url=https://academic.oup.com/book/45573/chapter-abstract/394740928?redirectedFrom=fulltext&login=false |title=Leveraging Latency: How the Weak Compel the Strong with Nuclear Technology |chapter=Chapter 6: Iran: Abstract |doi=10.1093/oso/9780197669532.003.0006 |publisher=[[Oxford University Press]] |quote=Iran developed its nuclear program from 1953 to 2002 and then botched a deception campaign before reconfiguring around a safer hedge posture.}}
 }}</ref> The ''[[Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists]]'' said that Iran was using its enrichment program and uranium stockpiles for leverage in international negotiations, and was willing to dilute or export its higher-level enriched uranium in exchange for sanctions relief and prevention of attacks.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |date=26 February 2026 |title=Give Negotiations a Chance: How to Turn Iran's Claimed Right to Enrich Uranium into a New Deal—and Avoid a War |url=https://thebulletin.org/2026/02/give-negotiations-a-chance-how-to-turn-irans-claimed-right-to-enrich-uranium-into-a-new-deal-and-avoid-a-war/ |access-date=5 March 2026 |website=Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists |language=en |quote=Iran has used its enrichment program for leverage in international negotiations.}}</ref>

==== Israel and nuclear weapons ====
{{Further|Israel and nuclear weapons|Middle East nuclear weapon free zone}}

Israel is [[Israel and nuclear weapons|widely believed to possess nuclear weapons]], with a stockpile estimated between 90 and 400 nuclear warheads.{{refn|There are a wide range of estimates as to the size of the Israeli nuclear arsenal. For a compiled list of estimates, see {{#invoke:cite |book |author=[[Avner Cohen]] |title=The Worst-Kept Secret: Israel's bargain with the Bomb |publisher=Columbia University Press |year=2010 |at=Table 1, p. xxvii and p. 82}}<ref name=HK>{{#invoke:cite |journal |last1=Kristensen |first1=Hans M. |last2=Norris |first2=Robert S. |year=2014 |title=Israeli Nuclear Weapons, 2014 |url=http://bos.sagepub.com/content/70/6/97.full.pdf+html |journal=[[Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists]] |volume=70 |number=6 |pages=97–115 |doi=10.1177/0096340214555409 |bibcode=2014BuAtS..70f..97K |doi-access=free}}</ref><ref name="israelhayom.com">{{#invoke:cite |news |last1=Hirsch |first1=Yoni |date=14 April 2014 |title=Carter Says Israel Has Stockpile of over 300 Nuclear Bombs |url=http://www.israelhayom.com/site/newsletter_article.php?id=16847 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140416210127/http://www.israelhayom.com/site/newsletter_article.php?id=16847 |archive-date=16 April 2014 |access-date=19 June 2024 |work=[[Israel Hayom]]}}</ref><ref name=independent-20160916>{{#invoke:cite |news |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/colin-powell-leaked-emails-nuclear-weapons-israel-iran-obama-deal-a7311626.html |title=Colin Powell Leaked Emails: Israel Has '200 Nukes All Pointed at Iran', Former US Secretary of State Says |last=Revesz |first=Rachael |newspaper=[[The Independent]] |date=16 September 2016 |access-date=10 May 2018}}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite |journal |last=Brower |first=Kenneth S |title=A Propensity for Conflict: Potential Scenarios and Outcomes of War in the Middle East |journal=[[Jane's Intelligence Review]] |type=special report |number=14 |date=February 1997 |pages=14–15}}</ref><ref name="armscontrol.org">{{#invoke:cite |web |title=Nuclear Weapons: Who Has What at a Glance |work=Arms Control Association |url=https://www.armscontrol.org/factsheets/Nuclearweaponswhohaswhat |access-date=24 September 2017}}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite |book |title=Israel and the Bomb |place=New York |publisher=[[Columbia University Press]] |last=Cohen |first=Avner |year=1998 |isbn=978-0-231-10482-1 |url=https://archive.org/details/israelbomb00cohe |page=349}}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |first=Mohamed |last=ElBaradei |url=http://www.iaea.org/NewsCenter/Transcripts/2004/alahram27072004.html |title=Transcript of the Director General's Interview with Al-Ahram News |author-link=Mohamed ElBaradei |website=[[International Atomic Energy Agency]] |date=27 July 2004 |access-date=3 June 2007}}</ref><ref>''My Promised Land'', by [[Ari Shavit]], (London 2014), page 188</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |title=Nuclear Weapons |website=FAS |url=https://fas.org/nuke/guide/israel/nuke/index.html |access-date=26 March 2026 |archive-date=7 December 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20101207122117/http://www.fas.org/nuke/guide/israel/nuke/index.html}}</ref><ref name="fas.org">{{#invoke:cite |web |url=https://fas.org/initiative/status-world-nuclear-forces/ |title=Status of World Nuclear Forces |website=Federation Of American Scientists}}</ref>}} Israel is the only [[List of states with nuclear weapons|nuclear-armed country]] that [[Israel and nuclear weapons#Policy|does not officially acknowledge]] its nuclear weapons capability.<ref name="Kristensen-2022">{{#invoke:cite |journal |last1=Kristensen |first1=Hans M. |last2=Korda |first2=Matt |date=2 January 2022 |title=Israeli Nuclear Weapons, 2021 |url=https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/00963402.2021.2014239 |journal=Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists |language=en |volume=78 |issue=1 |pages=38–50 |doi=10.1080/00963402.2021.2014239 |bibcode=2022BuAtS..78a..38K |issn=0096-3402 |url-access=subscription}}</ref> Some scholars have argued that Israel's nuclear weapons have incentivized Iran to develop its own nuclear program, seen as maintaining a balance of [[Deterrence theory|nuclear deterrence]], or causing a regional [[nuclear arms race]].<ref>{{#invoke:cite |journal |last=Cordesman |first=Anthony H. |date=9 March 2015 |title=Judging a P5+1 Agreement with Iran: The Key Technical and Strategic Issues |url=https://www.csis.org/analysis/judging-p51-agreement-iran-key-technical-and-strategic-issues |journal=[[Center for Strategic and International Studies]] |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite |journal |last=Maher |first=Nora |date=14 March 2020 |title=Balancing Deterrence: Iran-Israel Relations in a Turbulent Middle East |journal=Review of Economics and Political Science |volume=8 |issue=3 |pages=226–245 |doi=10.1108/reps-06-2019-0085 |issn=2356-9980 |hdl=10419/316081 |hdl-access=free}}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite |journal |last=Sariolghalam |first=Mahmood |date=2001 |title=Israeli-Turkish Military Cooperation: Iranian Perceptions and Responses |journal=Journal of Political & Military Sociology |volume=29 |issue=2 |pages=293–304 |jstor=45293807 |issn=0047-2697}}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |date=July 2008 |title=An Israel-Iran Balance of Nuclear Deterrence: Seeds of Instability |url=https://www.inss.org.il/publication/israel-iran-balance-nuclear-deterrence-seeds-instability/ |access-date=26 March 2026 |website=INSS |language=en-US}}</ref> Citing security threats, Israel rejects international calls to accede to the [[Treaty on the Non-Proliferation of Nuclear Weapons]] or to participate in negotiations of a [[Middle East nuclear weapon free zone]].<ref>{{Cite report |title=Narratives of the Middle East WMD-Free Zone: Drivers, Themes, And Historical Accounts |last=Sabet |first=Farzan |date=2023 |publisher=UNIDIR |doi=10.37559/mewmdfz/2023/narratives}}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |date=July 2022 |title=The Middle Eastern WMD-Free Zone and the NPT |url=https://www.armscontrol.org/act/2022-07/features/middle-eastern-wmd-free-zone-and-npt |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250620224839/https://www.armscontrol.org/act/2022-07/features/middle-eastern-wmd-free-zone-and-npt |archive-date=20 June 2025 |access-date=30 October 2025 |website=Arms Control Association |language=en |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="s316">{{#invoke:cite |news |date=29 May 2010 |title=Israel Rejects Call to Join Anti-Nuclear Treaty |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/world/israel-rejects-call-to-join-anti-nuclear-treaty-idUSTRE64S1ZN/ |access-date=30 October 2025 |work=[[Reuters]]}}</ref> This has frustrated both US<ref name="Kristensen-2022"/> and UK<ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |last=Curtis |first=Mark |date=23 March 2026 |title=UK Saw Israel as 'Chief Problem' to Curb Iran's Nuclear Ambitions |url=https://www.declassifieduk.org/uk-saw-israel-as-chief-problem-to-curb-irans-nuclear-ambitions/ |access-date=26 March 2026 |website=[[Declassified UK]] |language=en-US}}</ref> diplomatic efforts on such a zone. Scholars also suggest US tacit acceptance of the Israeli nuclear weapons capability is seen as a double standard that undermines its diplomatic credibility in Middle East [[nonproliferation]] negotiations.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |date=22 February 2024 |title=Nuclear Weapons and Non-Proliferation In the Middle East |url=https://carnegieendowment.org/sada/2024/02/nuclear-weapons-and-non-proliferation-in-the-middle-east |access-date=26 March 2026 |website=[[Carnegie Endowment for International Peace]] |language=en}}</ref><ref name="Kristensen-2022"/>

=== Prelude ===
