The initial dead included Iranian defense minister [[Aziz Nasirzadeh]], and four top [[Ministry of Intelligence (Iran)|Ministry of Intelligence]] officials.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |title=Four Senior Intelligence Ministry Officials Killed in Airstrikes on Iran |url=https://www.iranintl.com/en/202602284598 |website=[[Iran International]] |date=28 February 2026 |publisher=Volant Media UK Ltd |access-date=28 February 2026}}</ref>

Additional senior officials included [[Salah Asadi]], head of intelligence for Iran's emergency command, [[Mohammad Shirazi (general)|Mohammad Shirazi]], head of Khamenei's military office, head of the [[Organization of Defensive Innovation and Research]] (SPND) [[Hossein Jabal Amelian]], former SPND head [[Reza Mozaffari Nia]],<ref name="Iran International-2026">{{#invoke:cite |web |date=28 February 2026 |title=Live – Israel and US Launch Strikes on Iran as Tehran Prepares Retaliation |url=https://www.iranintl.com/en/liveblog/202602288143 |access-date=28 February 2026 |website=[[Iran International]] |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Zwartz |first=Henry |last2=wires |first2=Elissa Steedman with |date=19 March 2026 |title=Who in the Iranian leadership has been killed? |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-03-19/who-in-iranian-leadership-has-been-killed/106471816 |access-date=27 July 2026 |work=ABC News |language=en-AU}}</ref> and chief of staff [[Abdolrahim Mousavi]], among 40 official as reported by intelligence and military sources.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |last=LaPorta |first=James |date=28 February 2026 |title=About 40 Iranian Officials Killed in Strikes, Sources Say |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/live-updates/israel-us-attack-iran-trump-says-major-combat-operations/ |access-date=1 March 2026 |work=[[CBS News]] |language=en-US}}</ref>

On 13 March, IDF estimated that between 3,000 and 4,000 Iranian soldiers and commanders had been killed.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |date=13 March 2026 |title=Israeli Military Says 4,000–5,000 Iranian Forces Killed in Strikes so Far |url=https://www.iranintl.com/en/202603135117 |access-date=15 March 2026 |website=[[Iran International]] |language=en}}</ref>

On 17 March, Israel [[Assassination of Ali Larijani|assassinated Ali Larijani]], [[Secretary of the Supreme National Security Council|secretary of]] Iran's SNSC.<ref name="sk">{{#invoke:cite |news |date=17 March 2026 |title=Iran Confirms Security Chief Larijani, Basij Commander Soleimani Killed |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/3/17/israel-says-it-has-killed-ali-larijani-irans-top-security-official |access-date=17 March 2026 |work=[[Al Jazeera English]] |language=en}}</ref> IRGC commander [[Mohammad Pakpour]] and [[Iranian Defence Council|Defence Council]] secretary [[Ali Shamkhani]] were reported dead in late March.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |title='Decimated'? The Iranian Leaders Killed in Israeli-US War |url=https://www.france24.com/en/live-news/20260320-decimated-the-iranian-leaders-killed-in-israeli-us-war |access-date=29 March 2026 |agency=[[Agence France-Presse]] |work=[[France 24]] |date=20 March 2026}}</ref>

On 18 March, the [[Hengaw Organization for Human Rights]] estimated that 5,300+ members of the Iranian military had been killed.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |date=20 March 2026 |title=5,900 Killed in 21 Days of War, Including 595 Civilians: Hengaw's Sixth Report |url=https://hengaw.net/en/reports-and-statistics-1/2026/03/article-11 |access-date=20 March 2026 |website=Hengaw |language=en}}</ref>

On 31 March, [[Iran International]] reported that at least 4,700 security forces had been killed.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |date=31 March 2026 |title=Over 4,700 Security Forces Killed in US-Israeli Strikes on Iran |url=https://www.iranintl.com/en/202603313227 |access-date=31 March 2026 |website=Iran International |language=en}}</ref>

On 6 April, the head of IRGC intelligence, Major General Majid Khademi, was assassinated in a joint US-Israeli airstrike.<ref name="ii-khademi" />

As of 7 April, [[Human Rights Activists in Iran]] (HRANA), a US-based [[non-governmental organization]], reported 3,636 documented war deaths, including 1,701 civilians, 1,221 military personnel, and 714 unclassified. According to HRANA, "[i]t is believed that military casualties are significantly higher than the figures reported in [its] reports" as confirmations depend largely on government data. Its count largely reflects reports of military personnel present in urban areas.<ref name="HRANA">{{#invoke:cite |web |date=7 April 2026 |title=Day 39 of U.S. And Israeli Attacks on Iran: Extensive Damage to the Rail Network and Roads |url=https://www.en-hrana.org/day-39-of-u-s-and-israeli-attacks-on-iran-extensive-damage-to-the-rail-network-and-roads/ |access-date=7 April 2026 |website=HRANA |language=en}}</ref>

==== Civilians ====
{{Further|2026 Minab school attack|2026 Lamerd sports hall attack|2026 Karaj B1 bridge attack}}

[[File:Shajareh Tayyebeh school in Minab photos from Mehr (3).jpg|thumb|Rescuers and residents searching through the rubble of the [[2026 Minab school attack|Minab girls' elementary school]], destroyed by the United States]]
On the first day of the war, Iran reported that the Shajareh Tayyebeh girls' elementary school in [[Minab]] had been [[2026 Minab school attack|hit by an American attack]].<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |date=28 February 2026 |title=Over 50 Killed in Strike on Girls' Elementary School in Iran |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/video/newsfeed/2026/2/28/over-50-killed-in-strike-on-girls-elementary-school-in-iran |access-date=28 February 2026 |work=[[Al Jazeera English]] |language=en |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260228192239/https://www.aljazeera.com/video/newsfeed/2026/2/28/over-50-killed-in-strike-on-girls-elementary-school-in-iran |archive-date=28 February 2026}}</ref> The school was located near{{efn|Variously described as "about 600m [660 yd] from the base",<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |last1=Habibiazad |first1=Ghoncheh |last2=Greenall |first2=Robert |date=1 March 2026 |title=At Least 153 Dead After Reported Strike on School, Iran Says |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c1l7rvqq51eo |access-date=1 March 2026 |work=[[BBC News]] |language=en-GB |archive-date=1 March 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260301191602/https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c1l7rvqq51eo |url-status=live}}</ref> "within less than 100 yards [91 m] of the perimeter of [the base]",<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |last1=Brumfiel |first1=Geoff |last2=Hurt |first2=Alyson |date=4 March 2026 |title=Satellite Images Show Iran School Strike Hit More Buildings Than Earlier Reported |url=https://www.npr.org/2026/03/04/nx-s1-5735801/satellite-imagery-shows-strike-that-destroyed-iranian-school-was-more-extensive-than-first-reported |access-date=8 March 2026 |work=[[NPR]]}}</ref> "adjacent to [the base]"<ref name="Browne-2026">{{#invoke:cite |news |last1=Browne |first1=Malachy |last2=Livni |first2=Ephrat |last3=Mahoozi |first3=Sanam |date=1 March 2026 |title=Strike on Girls' School Kills at Least 175, Iranian State Media Says |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2025/03/01/world/middleeast/girls-school-strike-iran-video.html |access-date=3 March 2026 |work=[[The New York Times]]}}</ref>}} the Sayyid al-Shuhada military complex,<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |date=3 March 2026 |title=Al Jazeera Investigation: Iran Girls' School Targeting Likely 'Deliberate' |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/3/3/questions-over-minab-girls-school-strike-as-israel-us-deny-involvement |access-date=3 March 2026 |work=[[Al Jazeera English]] |language=en}}</ref> of which it had once been a part.<ref name="Browne-2026"/><ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |last1=Angelovski |first1=Ivan |last2=Szeto |first2=Eric |last3=Bilhete |first3=Britnei |date=4 March 2026 |title=Who Bombed a Girls' School in Iran? A Visual Investigation |url=https://www.cbc.ca/news/world/iran-school-bombing-investigation-9.7114994 |access-date=5 March 2026 |work=[[CBC News]] |language=en}}</ref> While the number killed was not independently confirmed,<ref name="BBC News-2026b">{{#invoke:cite |news |date=28 February 2026 |title=US and Israel Launch Attack on Iran, As Trump Says 'Major Combat Operations' Under Way |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/live/cn5ge95q6y7t |access-date=28 February 2026 |work=[[BBC News]] |language=en-GB}}</ref> Iranian state media reported that more than 175 people, mostly children, were killed while 95 were wounded.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |last1=Copp |first1=Tara |last2=Mekhennet |first2=Souad |author-link2=Souad Mekhennet |last3=Kelly |first3=Meg |last4=Horton |first4=Alex |last5=Geroge |first5=Susannah |date=11 March 2026 |title=Iranian School Was on U.S. Target List, May Have Been Mistaken as Military Site |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/national-security/2026/03/11/us-strike-iran-elementary-school-ai-target-list/ |access-date=14 March 2026 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]}}</ref> Video showed a Tomahawk missile hitting a building adjacent to the school;<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |last1=McClure |first1=Tess |date=9 March 2026 |title=Video Shows US Tomahawk Missile Hit Base Next to Bombed Iranian School |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/mar/09/video-shows-us-tomahawk-missile-hit-base-next-bombed-iranian-school |work=[[The Guardian]] |access-date=9 March 2026}}</ref> preliminary findings from an American military investigation indicated that the strike resulted from outdated targeting data.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |date=8 March 2026 |title=United States Was "Likely" Responsible for Bombing of Girls' School in Iran, Per Early U.S. Assessment |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/us-iran-war-bombing-girls-school-assessment/ |access-date=9 March 2026 |work=[[CBS News]] |language=en-US}}</ref>

At least 20 civilians, mostly families who gathered after breaking their [[Islamic fasting|fast]] at Ramadan,<ref name="BBCd4xo">{{#invoke:cite |web |last1=Al-Maghafi |first1=Nawal |last2=Akgun |first2=Ruzgar |last3=Jhaveri |first3=Ishaan |last4=Costard |first4=Emile |date=27 March 2026 |title=Iran War: Inside Tehran as the Civilian Toll of US-Israeli Strikes Rises |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cn0w1qxzd4xo |access-date=28 March 2026 |website=BBC |language=en-GB}}</ref> were killed in Tehran's Niloofar Square on 1 or 2 March, according to Iranian-state media.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |url=https://aje.news/wwomw4?update=4353612 |title=Iranian Media Say 20 Killed in Attack on Tehran's Niloofar Square |date=2 March 2026 |work=[[Al Jazeera English]]}}</ref> On 3 March, the [[Iranian Red Crescent Society]] said that over 600 civilians had been killed<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/3/2/iran-death-toll-reaches-555-as-us-israel-escalate-attacks |title=Iran Death Toll Reaches 555 as US, Israel Escalate Attacks |date=2 March 2026 |work=[[Al Jazeera English]]}}</ref> while HRANA reported 1,097 civilian deaths.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.en-hrana.org/the-fourth-day-of-the-u-s-israel-war-on-iran-strikes-continue-in-western-regions-of-the-country/ |title=The Fourth Day of the U.S.–Israel War on Iran: Strikes Continue in Western Regions of the Country |work=HRANA |date=4 March 2026}}</ref> On 7 March, the Red Crescent reported that over 6,668 civilian "units" had been targeted by US-Israeli strikes, including 5,535 residential units, 1,041 commercial units, 14 medical centers, 65 schools, and 13 centers affiliated with the Red Crescent.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |date=7 March 2026 |title=US-Israeli Strikes Targeted at Least 6,668 Civilian Units: Red Crescent |url=https://aje.news/ozn6u2?update=4374430 |work=[[Al Jazeera English]]}}</ref> The Middle East Eye, an anti-Israel news outlet, reported that the US used [[Double tap strike|double tap]] airstrikes,<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |date=4 March 2026 |title=Exclusive: Iranian Girls Killed by 'Double-Tap' Strikes on Minab School |url=https://www.middleeasteye.net/news/exclusive-iranian-girls-killed-double-tap-strikes-minab-school |work=[[Middle East Eye]] |access-date=8 March 2026 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |date=8 March 2026 |title=Double-Tap Missile Attack Kills 20 in Iran's Najafabad: Report |url=https://www.middleeasteye.net/live-blog/live-blog-update/report-double-tap-missile-attack-kills-20-irans-najafabad |work=[[Middle East Eye]] |access-date=8 March 2026 |language=en}}</ref> although the US denies this. As of 23 March, HRANA estimated that at least 15% of the casualties were under age 18.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |date=22 March 2026 |title=Day 23 of U.S. And Israeli Attacks on Iran: More Than 15% of the Total Fatalities Are Children |url=https://www.en-hrana.org/day-23-of-u-s-and-israeli-attacks-on-iran-more-than-15-of-the-total-fatalities-are-children/ |website=Human Rights Activists News Agency}}</ref>

An Israeli airstrike on 9 March in [[Resalat (district)|Resalat]] neighbourhood of Tehran destroyed a [[Basij]]-affiliated building as well as three residential buildings, killing 40 to 50. According to a BBC analysis, Israel used [[Mark 82]] bombs in the attack.<ref name="BBCd4xo"/>

As of 27 March, Iran has reported damage to at least 120 [[History of Iran|historical sites]].<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |date=27 March 2026 |title=Iran Says US, Israeli Strikes Damage 120 Museums, Historic Buildings |url=https://english.aawsat.com/node/5255868 |access-date=28 March 2026 |work=[[Asharq Al-Awsat]]}}</ref>

The [[2026 Lamerd sports hall attack|deadly attack on residential buildings and a sports hall]] in the town of [[Lamerd]] killed 21 including 4 children and injured 100.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |title=Video Shows US Missile Likely Used in Deadly Strike on Iranian Town, Experts Say |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cx29mzmwdw2o |access-date=29 March 2026 |work=BBC |date=28 March 2026}}</ref>

==== Institutions ====
The initial attack targeted Leadership House. On 2 March, video footage released by Iran International showed the destroyed IRGC Malek-Ashtar building in Tehran following a joint US-Israel missile strike.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |date=2 March 2026 |title=Video Shows US-Israel Strike Destroys IRGC's Malek-Ashtar Building |url=https://www.iranintl.com/en/202603022715 |access-date=3 March 2026 |website=[[Iran International]] |language=en}}</ref> On 3 March, the complex of [[Islamic Republic of Iran Broadcasting]] (IRIB), the state broadcaster's headquarters, was hit in an Israeli air operation.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |date=16 June 2025 |title=Israel Bombs Iran's State TV After Threatening It Would 'Disappear' |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2025/6/16/israel-bombs-irans-state-tv-after-threatening-it-would |work=[[Al Jazeera English]] |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |title=IRIB Struck by Israel, Benjamin Netanyahu Hijacks Transmission |url=https://www.jpost.com/middle-east/article-888487 |work=[[The Jerusalem Post]] |date=2 March 2026 |language=en}}</ref>

Iranian state-linked media indicated that Iran's parliament building was targeted.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |date=2 March 2026 |title=Iran's Parliament Building Likely Targeted in Airstrikes – IRGC Outlet |url=https://www.iranintl.com/en/202603027136 |access-date=3 March 2026 |website=[[Iran International]] |language=en}}</ref> On 3 March, Israeli forces bombed the [[Assembly of Experts]] office building in [[Qom]].<ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |last=Ravid |first=Barak |date=3 March 2026 |title=Israel Bombs Council Choosing Iran's Next Supreme Leader, Official Says |url=https://www.axios.com/2026/03/03/iran-supreme-leader-council-israel-strike |access-date=3 March 2026 |website=[[Axios (website)|Axios]] |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |date=3 March 2026 |title=Clerical Body in Charge of Choosing Iran's Next Leader Hit in Qom and Tehran |url=https://www.iranintl.com/en/202603031369 |access-date=3 March 2026 |website=[[Iran International]] |language=en}}</ref>

==== Infrastructure ====
By 5 March, the World Health Organization had identified 13 Iranian health sites hit, as well as one in Lebanon.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |date=5 March 2026 |title=WHO Says It Has Verified 13 Attacks on Health Sites in Iran |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/middle-east/who-says-has-it-has-verified-13-health-attacks-iran-2026-03-05/ |work=[[Reuters]]}}</ref> The Iranian Foreign Minister said that the US [[2026 Qeshm Island desalination plant attack|had struck a desalination plant]] on [[Qeshm Island]], disrupting water supply to 30 villages, but both the US and Israel denied an attack.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |date=7 March 2026 |title=US Hit Desalination Plant on Qeshm Island, Iran FM Says |url=https://www.iranintl.com/en/202603072697 |access-date=13 March 2026 |website=[[Iran International]] |language=en}}</ref><ref name="b673"/> The [[List of diplomatic missions of Russia#Asia|Russian consulate in Isfahan]] was damaged.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |date=11 March 2026 |title=Russian Consulate in Iran's Isfahan Damaged in Strikes, Moscow Says |url=https://www.trtworld.com/article/caf1ab154b6a |access-date=12 March 2026 |work=[[TRT World]] |language=en}}</ref> After US strikes on 9 June, Iran said that damage to water storage units in Hormozgan province had left 20,000 people without water.<ref name="GhaffariFT2026_06_09"/>

On 18 July, Iranian media reported that U.S. strikes hit power facilities and desalination pumps at the Bonji desalination plant in Jask, Hormozgan Province. Reuters reported that several missiles struck the facilities, citing a local official, and said the attack disrupted water supplies to 20 villages, affecting around 10,000 people.<ref>{{Cite web |date=18 July 2026 |title=Iran renews attacks on Gulf states after another night of US strikes |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/middle-east/iran-renews-attacks-gulf-states-after-another-night-us-strikes-2026-07-18/ |website=Reuters}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=18 July 2026 |title=تهاجم دشمن به آب‌شیرین‌کن بونجی جاسک/ ۲۰ روستا بی‌آب شدند |url=https://www.irna.ir/news/86211896/%D8%AA%D9%87%D8%A7%D8%AC%D9%85-%D8%AF%D8%B4%D9%85%D9%86-%D8%A8%D9%87-%D8%A2%D8%A8-%D8%B4%DB%8C%D8%B1%DB%8C%D9%86-%DA%A9%D9%86-%D8%A8%D9%88%D9%86%D8%AC%DB%8C-%D8%AC%D8%A7%D8%B3%DA%A9-%DB%B2%DB%B0-%D9%87%D8%B2%D8%A7%D8%B1-%D8%B1%D9%88%D8%B3%D8%AA%D8%A7-%D8%A8%DB%8C-%D8%A2%D8%A8-%D8%B4%D8%AF%D9%86%D8%AF |access-date=18 July 2026 |website=www.irna.ir |language=fa}}</ref>

=== American losses ===

Analysis of satellite images by ''[[The Washington Post]]'' revealed damage to at least 228 structures or pieces of equipment at US bases across the Persian Gulf, far more than the Trump administration has admitted to.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hill |first1=Evan |last2=Ley |first2=Jarrett |last3=Horton |first3=Alex |last4=Copp |first4=Tara |last5=Lamothe |first5=Dan |author5-link=Dan Lamothe |title=Iran Has Hit Far More U.S. Military Assets Than Reported, Satellite Images Show |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/investigations/2026/05/06/iran-us-bases-satellite-images/ |newspaper=The Washington Post |date=6 May 2026}}</ref>

On 1 March, six US soldiers were killed amid more than 30 injured in an Iranian drone attack near [[Camp Arifjan]] in Port Shuaiba, [[Kuwait]]. Those killed were soldiers of the [[United States Army Reserve]] assigned to the [[103rd Expeditionary Sustainment Command]], headquartered in [[Des Moines, Iowa]].<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |last1=Kelly |first1=Meg |last2=Horton |first2=Alex |last3=Ley |first3=Jarrett |date=4 March 2026 |title=U.S. Troops Had Little Protection from Drone Strike That Killed 6, Imagery Shows |url=https://www.msn.com/en-us/news/world/us-troops-had-little-protection-from-drone-strike-that-killed-six-imagery-shows/ar-AA1Xug33?apiversion=v2&domshim=1&noservercache=1&noservertelemetry=1&batchservertelemetry=1&renderwebcomponents=1&wcseo=1 |access-date=11 March 2026 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |language=en-US |issn=0190-8286 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260325132717/https://www.msn.com/en-us/news/world/us-troops-had-little-protection-from-drone-strike-that-killed-six-imagery-shows/ar-AA1Xug33?apiversion=v2&domshim=1&noservercache=1&noservertelemetry=1&batchservertelemetry=1&renderwebcomponents=1&wcseo=1 |archive-date=25 March 2026 |url-status=live |via=[[MSN]]}}</ref> On 8 March, [[CENTCOM]] reported that a [[National Guard (United States)|National Guard]] soldier died during a medical emergency.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |last=Mitchell |first=Ellen |date=9 March 2026 |title=US Military Reviewing National Guard Member's Death in Kuwait in Health-Related Incident |url=https://thehill.com/policy/defense/5774793-nypd-officer-kuwait-incident |work=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]]}}</ref> Sgt. Benjamin N. Pennington, a soldier of the [[1st Space Brigade]] wounded in an attack against [[Prince Sultan Air Base]] in Saudi Arabia on 1 March, died from his injuries on 9 March.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |url=https://www.spacecom.mil/Newsroom/News/Article-Display/Article/4428548/statement-by-commander-of-united-states-space-command-on-loss-of-usasmdc-soldie/ |title=Statement by Commander of United States Space Command on Loss of USASMDC Soldier in Support of Operation Epic Fury |date=9 March 2026 |website=[[United States Space Command]]}}</ref>

Six American airmen were killed on 12 March when their [[KC-135]] aerial refueling aircraft crashed in western Iraq. CENTCOM said that the crash was not the result of hostile action, but had resulted during an incident involving another US aircraft. By 13 March, the US military publicly announced that about 140 American servicemen had been injured.<ref name="AP1">{{#invoke:cite |news |url=https://apnews.com/article/us-iran-tanker-aircraft-crash-iraq-kc135-c337359a58be6280dc96fdbf1cb48a5b |title=All 6 Crew Members on a US Refueling Plane That Crashed in Iraq Are Dead, US Military Says |first1=Konstantin |last1=Toropin |first2=Ben |last2=Finley |first3=Kim |last3=Tong-Hyung |work=[[Associated Press]] |date=13 March 2026 |access-date=14 March 2026}}</ref> A 27 March attack on Prince Sultan Air Base damaged "multiple" refueling aircraft and destroyed a [[Boeing E-3 Sentry]], as well as wounding at least 15 US soldiers.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |last=Gordon |first=Chris |date=27 March 2026 |title=US Forces at Saudi Air Base Suffer Iranian Attack |url=https://www.airandspaceforces.com/us-forces-saudi-arabia-iran-attack/ |access-date=6 April 2026 |website=Air & Space Forces Magazine |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |last=Rogoway |first=Tyler |date=29 March 2026 |title=Images Show E-3 Sentry Totally Destroyed From Iranian Strike (Updated) |url=https://www.twz.com/air/images-purportedly-show-e-3-sentry-totally-destroyed-from-iranian-strike |access-date=6 April 2026 |website=The War Zone |language=en-US}}</ref><ref name="apnews27march"/>

The {{USS|Gerald R. Ford}}, the largest aircraft carrier in theatre, was damaged by a fire that broke out 12 March. The [[US Navy]] said the fire had injured multiple sailors,{{efn|name=Ford|The U.S. Navy initially said two sailors were treated for non-life-threatening injuries, then acknowledged that a third had been medically evacuated for further treatment.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |url=https://www.stripes.com/branches/navy/2026-03-16/ford-fire-red-sea-iran-war-21082703.html |title=Third Injured Sailor Flown off USS Gerald R. Ford After Fire Unrelated to Combat |first=Alison |last=Bath |work=Stars and Stripes |date=16 March 2026 |access-date=20 March 2026}}</ref> Several media outlets reported that as many as 200 sailors were also treated for symptoms of smoke inhalation.<ref name="usni170326">{{#invoke:cite |news |url=https://news.usni.org/2026/03/17/uss-gerald-r-ford-headed-to-souda-bay-for-repairs-after-fire |work=USNI |title=USS Gerald R. Ford Headed to Souda Bay for Repairs After Fire |date=17 March 2026 |access-date=20 March 2026 |first1=Mallory |last1=Shelbourne |first2=Sam |last2=Lagrone}}</ref><ref name="independent190326">{{#invoke:cite |news |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/uss-gerald-r-ford-warship-fire-crete-b2941151.html |work=[[The Independent]] |title=Trump's Biggest Warship USS Gerald R Ford Limps to Crete for Repairs After Huge Fire |first=Maryam |last=Zakir-Hussain |date=19 March 2026 |access-date=20 March 2026}}</ref><ref name="guardian180326">{{#invoke:cite |news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/mar/18/onboard-fire-uss-gerald-ford-aircraft-carrier |title=Fire Damage, Clogged Toilets, And Sinking Morale: USS Gerald R Ford to Set Sail for Repairs in Crete |first=Mark |last=Saunokonoko |date=18 March 2026 |access-date=20 March 2026 |work=[[The Guardian]]}}</ref>}} started in a laundry area and was not combat-related.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |url=https://www.cusnc.navy.mil/Media/News/Display/Article/4431969/statement-of-uss-gerald-r-ford/ |title=Statement of USS Gerald R. Ford |date=12 March 2026 |access-date=20 March 2026 |website=[[U.S. Navy]]}}</ref> The carrier had been dealing with other mechanical issues, including a malfunctioning sewage treatment system, since before it arrived in the Middle East.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |url=https://www.npr.org/2026/03/17/nx-s1-5746006/uss-ford-iran |work=[[NPR]] |title=USS Ford Has Seen War, Fire and Plumbing Woes as It Nears a Record Long Deployment |first=Steve |last=Walsh |date=17 March 2026 |access-date=20 March 2026}}</ref> According to multiple reports on 17–18 March, ''Gerald R. Ford'' paused launching airstrikes and sailed toward the [[Crete Naval Base]] for repairs.<ref name="usni170326"/>

Many US military bases near Iran were rendered "all but uninhabitable" due to Iranian strikes. Within the  first two weeks, Iran's attacks had caused $800 million in damage. Affected bases include [[Al-Sader base|Al-Sader]] and [[Al-Ruwais base|Al-Ruwais]] in the UAE, the [[Naval Support Activity Bahrain|naval base in Bahrain]], [[Ali Al Salem Air Base|Ali Al-Salem]] in Kuwait, [[Al Udeid Air Base|Al-Udeid]] in Qatar, and [[Prince Sultan Air Base|Prince Sultan]] in Saudi Arabia. According to unnamed officials, the damage degraded the US's military ability in the war. Many US servicemembers relocated to "hotels and office spaces", which raised concerns of the US using civilians as [[human shields]]. An attack on a hotel in Bahrain injured two Pentagon employees.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |last1=Zhang |first1=Sharon |title=As Trump Strikes Victorious Tone, Report Says Iran Rendered Many US Bases "All But Uninhabitable" |url=https://truthout.org/articles/report-many-middle-east-us-bases-all-but-uninhabitable-due-to-iran-strikes/ |work=[[Truthout]] |date=26 March 2026}}</ref>

Based on numbers given by members of the United States Congress, by May 2026 the United States military had lost 39 aircraft in "Operation Epic Fury".<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news|title=US Congressman Admits 39 Aircraft Losses in Iran War: F-35, AWACS and Reaper Attrition Sparks Questions Over America's Airpower Strategy |url=https://defencesecurityasia.com/en/us-congressman-39-aircraft-losses-iran-war-f35-awacs-reaper-airpower/|work=Defense Security Asia |date=14 May 2026}}</ref>

On 20–21 July 2026, [[The New York Times]] reported that the Pentagon had not publicly disclosed three Iranian strikes against U.S. forces in [[Jordan]] that occurred before the 17 July attack, nor the casualties and damage resulting from them. According to the newspaper, the earlier attacks injured dozens of U.S. service members and damaged several helicopters. The Pentagon said it was not required to publicly release information about U.S. military injuries and denied concealing casualty figures, with Pentagon spokesperson [[Sean Parnell]] stating that casualty information was publicly available through the Defense Casualty Analysis System (DCAS). On 21 July, The Intercept reported that the Pentagon acknowledged that nearly 100 U.S. service members had been injured in Iranian attacks since 7 July. According to the report, Parnell said that the vast majority of the injuries were minor concussions, that 96 percent of affected personnel had returned to duty, and that casualty information would be updated through the DCAS.

