== Direct impacts ==

=== United States ===
The United States consumed more than half of its THAAD and Patriot missiles used to intercept Iranian missiles and drones. The stockpiles limitation has reportedly turned into a key determinant for continuation or expansion of the conflict.{{efn|Over the last two years, the US military has deployed reserves across multiple fronts, including countering Houthi attacks in the Red Sea, defending Israel from Iranian missiles, assisting Ukraine, and protecting US troops in the Middle East. This extensive deployment has led to rapid consumption of advanced interceptor missiles, raising concerns among experts about the ability of [[List of United States defense contractors|defense companies]] to replenish stocks. Replacing critical munitions will take time, as will increasing supplies for potential future conflicts, such as [[Davidson window|with China in the Taiwan Strait]].}}<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=https://www.bbc.com/persian/articles/cn8n0e3e94yo|title=آیا جنگ ایران انبار موشک‌های رهگیر آمریکا را خالی کرده است؟|website=[[BBC News Persian]]|date=27 July 2026|language=fa}}</ref> In July 2026, the US awarded [[Lockheed Martin]] a record $58.6 billion contract to manufacture Patriot missile interceptors, as the [[Middle Eastern crisis (2023–present)|Middle Eastern crisis]] and the [[Russo-Ukrainian war (2022-present)|Russo-Ukrainian war]] reduced global US military armaments stocks.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=https://www.timesofisrael.com/liveblog_entry/us-awards-lockheed-martin-a-record-58-6b-contract-to-produce-patriot-missile-interceptors/|title=US awards Lockheed Martin a record $58.6B contract to produce Patriot missile interceptors|website=[[The Times of Israel]]|date=30 July 2026}}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=https://thehill.com/policy/defense/6000739-lockheed-martin-contract-patriot-interceptor-missiles-stockpile/|title=Lockheed Martin wins $58.6B contract to produce Patriot interceptors|website=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]]|date=30 July 2026}}</ref> As of August 4, the US reportedly has consumed nearly all of its long-range precision missiles during the war.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=https://www.reuters.com/world/us-has-used-virtually-all-its-long-range-precision-missiles-during-iran-war-2026-08-04/|title=US has used 'virtually all' of its long-range precision missiles during Iran war, sources say|website=[[Reuters]]|date=4 August 2026}}</ref>

=== Iran ===
{{main|Impact of the 2026 Iran war on Iran}}

==== Leadership ====
{{see also|2026 Iranian supreme leader election}}
{{#invoke:multiple image|
| image1       = Masoud Pezeshkian 2025 (cropped).jpg
| image2       = Gholam Hossein Mohseni-Eje'i 2023 (Cropped).jpg
| image3       = Alireza Arafi 13990824 0146818 crop.jpg
| perrow       = 3
| footer       = The three members of the [[Interim Leadership Council]]{{snd}}President [[Masoud Pezeshkian]], Chief Justice [[Gholam-Hossein Mohseni-Eje'i]] and Ayatollah [[Alireza Arafi]]
| total_width  = 350}}

The death of Ali Khamenei triggered an election for a new supreme leader. Under the terms of Iran's constitution, the [[Interim Leadership Council]] was established on 1 March to exercise the functions of Iran's [[head of state]] until a new supreme leader is elected.<ref name="Reuters-2026"/>

[[Mojtaba Khamenei]] was elected on 8 March 2026 to replace his father as supreme leader,<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |last=Tondo |first=Lorenzo |date=8 March 2026 |title=Ali Khamenei's Son Mojtaba Chosen as Iran's New Supreme Leader |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/mar/08/ali-khameneis-son-mojtaba-chosen-as-irans-new-supreme-leader |access-date=8 March 2026 |work=[[The Guardian]] |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref> and the IRGC, as well as Iran's top leaders, including [[Mohammad Bagher Ghalibaf]], [[Ali Larijani]], and [[Masoud Pezeshkian]],<ref name="Sabah-2026">{{#invoke:cite |news |last=Sabah |first=Zaid |date=9 March 2026 |title=Iran Names Mojtaba Khamenei to Replace His Father as Supreme Leader |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/liveblog/2026/3/9/iran-war-live-mojtaba-khamenei-named-supreme-leader-israel-bombs-tehran |access-date=9 March 2026 |work=[[Al Jazeera English]] |language=en}}</ref> pledged their allegiance to him.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |last=Kelliher |first=Fiona |date=8 March 2026 |title='Act of Defiance': Iran Picks Khamenei's Son to Lead as US-Israel Attack |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/liveblog/2026/3/8/iran-live-israel-bombs-tehran-oil-depots-attacks-on-gulf-states-continue |access-date=8 March 2026 |work=[[Al Jazeera English]] |language=en}}</ref>

In late March, the NYT described Iran's leadership as paralyzed with severely disrupted decision-making process. It also reported that the damage to communications infrastructure caused paranoia and internal power struggles.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |title=Iran's Leadership Is Struggling to Function and Facing Mounting Internal Disarray - Report |url=https://www.i24news.tv/en/news/middle-east/iran-eastern-states/artc-iran-s-leadership-is-struggling-to-function-and-facing-mounting-internal-disarray-report |access-date=2 April 2026 |work=[[i24NEWS]] |date=31 March 2026 |language=en}}</ref>

As of 21 May, Khamenei remains in hiding and the de facto ruler is uncertain. The IRGC is currently under command of the hardliner general [[Ahmad Vahidi]],<ref>{{cite news |last1=GAMBRELL |first1=JON |title=This Hard-Line Iranian General Is a Major Player in Talks with US over War |url=https://apnews.com/article/iran-war-us-negotiations-ahmad-vahidi-0905fc9612407e75fffbfd0280776692 |work=Associated Press |date=21 May 2026 |language=en}}</ref> who is cited as the formulator of Iran's military and negotiation stance.<ref>{{cite news |title=U.S. Think Tank: Ahmad Vahidi, Key Figure Behind Iran's Hardline Stance in Negotiations |url=https://iranwire.com/en/news/152695-us-think-tank-ahmad-vahidi-key-figure-behind-irans-hardline-stance-in-negotiations/ |work=IranWire |date=21 May 2026 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Irans General Ahmad Vahidi Ist Der Neue Starke Mann Im Mullah-Regime - Und Ein Problem Für Trump |url=https://www.fr.de/politik/fuer-trump-irans-regime-veraendert-sich-und-der-mann-der-uebernimmt-ist-ein-problem-zr-94288507.html |work=[[Frankfurter Rundschau]] |date=3 May 2026 |language=de}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Ahmed Vahidi: Der De-Facto-Herrscher Des Iranischen Regimes |url=https://www.mena-watch.com/ahmed-vahidi-de-facto-herrscher-iran-regime/ |work=Mena-Watch |date=23 May 2026 |language=de}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Kerl |first1=Christian |title=Geheimplan Für Den Machtwechsel Im Iran Gescheitert – Nun Hat Trump Ein Problem |url=https://www.morgenpost.de/politik/article412057987/geheimplan-fuer-den-machtwechsel-im-iran-gescheitert-nun-hat-trump-ein-problem.html |work=[[Berliner Morgenpost]] |date=21 May 2026 |language=de}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Associated Press: This Hard-Line Iranian General Is a Major Player in Talks with US over War |url=https://understandingwar.org/newsroom/associated-press-this-hard-line-iranian-general-is-a-major-player-in-talks-with-us-over-war/ |work=Institute for the Study of War |date=21 May 2026}}</ref> ''The Telegraph'' has revealed a shadowy partnership with the retired IRGC commander-in-chief [[Mohammad Ali Jafari]].<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/2026/05/29/shadowy-new-hardline-alliance-reshaping-irans-regime/|title=Shadowy new hardline alliance reshaping Iran's regime|website=[[The Daily Telegraph]]|date=29 May 2026}}</ref>

==== Civilian life ====
{{see also|2025–2026 Iranian protests#After the strikes}}

[[File:Tehran - The Fourth Day of War 4 Avash.webp|thumb|Residents in Tehran on the third day of US-Israeli airstrikes, 3 March 2026]]
According to UN Human Rights Chief [[Volker Türk]], the "reckless" war is disproportionately impacting civilians in the Middle East and beyond.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |date=19 March 2026 |title=Civilians Bear Brunt of Reckless War in the Middle East, Says Türk |url=https://www.ohchr.org/en/press-releases/2026/03/civilians-bear-brunt-reckless-war-middle-east-says-turk |website=[[OHCHR]] |language=en}}</ref>

138,000 civilian sites in Iran were damaged during the conflict, with estimated time of reconstruction being 2-5 years.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Ramazanian |first=Ali |date=24 April 2026 |script-title=fa:بازسازی ویرانه‌های جنگ در ایران چگونه پیش می‌رود؟ |title= |trans-title=How is the reconstruction of war ruins progressing in Iran? |url=https://www.bbc.com/persian/articles/cz0evpdrzxro |access-date=26 April 2026 |work=[[BBC News]] Persian |language=fa}}</ref>

As the US and Israel executed joint strikes targeting Iranian military and institutional targets, Iran's capital reportedly became a [[ghost town]], as civilians feared going outside or to their daily jobs.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |date=3 March 2026 |title=Bombardment Unleashes Terror in Tehran with No Sign of Protests |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/middle-east/bombardment-unleashes-terror-tehran-with-no-sign-protests-2026-03-03 |work=[[Reuters]]}}</ref> Reports have emerged that prisoners in [[Evin Prison]] have been receiving limited bread and water since the onset of the war.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |date=4 March 2026 |title=Families Say Food Distribution Disrupted in Evin Wards After Strikes in Tehran |url=https://www.iranintl.com/en/202603043283 |access-date=5 March 2026 |website=[[Iran International]] |language=en}}</ref> After the strikes, schools were ordered closed while banks and government offices remained in operation at reduced capacity. Local media reported that subway and bus services would remain operational.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |date=28 February 2026 |title=Streets Empty and Shops Close as US Strikes Confirm Iranian Fears |url=https://www.france24.com/en/live-news/20260228-streets-empty-and-shops-close-as-us-strikes-confirm-iranian-fears |access-date=17 March 2026 |work=[[France 24]] |language=en}}</ref>

During the "near total" [[2026 Internet blackout in Iran|internet blackout in Iran]], [[NetBlocks]] reported that internet connectivity dropped to 4% of ordinary levels.<ref name="BBC News-2026b"/><ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |url=https://mastodon.social/@netblocks/116147264437940657 |title=NetBlocks (@netblocks@mastodon.social) |date=28 February 2026 |website=Mastodon}}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |url=https://www.iranintl.com/fa/202602281170 |script-title=fa:نت‌بلاکس از خاموشی اینترنت در ایران خبر داد |title= |trans-title=NetBlocks reported an internet blackout in Iran |date=28 February 2026 |website=[[Iran International]] |language=fa}}</ref> As the internet blackout exceeded 240 hours, making it the second longest ever, the government started handing out "white sim cards", which bypass filters, to government supporters, in order to allow them to promote its messaging.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |date=10 March 2026 |title=Iran Keeps Loyal Voices Online as Public Faces Record Internet Blackout |url=https://www.iranintl.com/en/202603106004 |access-date=11 March 2026 |website=[[Iran International]] |language=en}}</ref> As news of Khamenei's death broke, security forces were deployed to prevent an uprising, with footage showing them opening fire on celebrants in the streets<ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |url=https://www.iranintl.com/fa/202602280827 |script-title=fa:حضور نیروهای یگان ویژه در جنت‌آباد تهران |title= |trans-title=The presence of special forces in Jannat Abad, Tehran |date=28 February 2026 |website=[[Iran International]] |language=fa}}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |script-title=fa:تیراندازی و جولان سرکوبگران در سبزوار؛ شادی مردم پس از انتشار خبر مرگ علی خامنه‌ای |title= |trans-title=Shooting and the rampage of suppressors in Sabzevar; the joy of people after the news of Ali Khamenei's death was released |url=https://www.independentpersian.com/tv/%D8%A7%DB%8C%D8%B1%D8%A7%D9%86/%D8%AA%DB%8C%D8%B1%D8%A7%D9%86%D8%AF%D8%A7%D8%B2%DB%8C-%D9%88-%D8%AC%D9%88%D9%84%D8%A7%D9%86-%D8%B3%D8%B1%DA%A9%D9%88%D8%A8%DA%AF%D8%B1%D8%A7%D9%86-%D8%AF%D8%B1-%D8%B3%D8%A8%D8%B2%D9%88%D8%A7%D8%B1%D8%9B-%D8%B4%D8%A7%D8%AF%DB%8C-%D9%85%D8%B1%D8%AF%D9%85-%D9%BE%D8%B3-%D8%A7%D8%B2-%D8%A7%D9%86%D8%AA%D8%B4%D8%A7%D8%B1-%D8%AE%D8%A8%D8%B1-%D9%85%D8%B1%DA%AF-%D8%B9%D9%84%DB%8C-%D8%AE%D8%A7%D9%85%D9%86%D9%87%E2%80%8C%D8%A7%DB%8C-2EaOQHTL |access-date=1 March 2026 |website=[[Independent Persian]] |language=fa}}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |last=Sinaiee |first=Maryam |date=1 March 2026 |title=Iranians React with Joy and Disbelief to Khamenei's Death |url=https://www.iranintl.com/en/202602288551 |access-date=1 March 2026 |website=[[Iran International]] |language=en}}</ref> and shooting at people chanting behind the windows of their homes.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |url=https://www.iranintl.com/en/202603048590 |title=Security Forces Shoot at People Chanting Behind Windows |date=4 March 2026 |website=[[Iran International]]}}</ref>

During the first two weeks of March 2026, over 70,000 [[Afghans in Iran|Afghan refugees]] returned to Afghanistan, frequently under forced circumstances, exacerbating the country's [[2025–2026 hunger crisis in Afghanistan|severe humanitarian crisis]].<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |last1=Peltier |first1=Elian |title=Caught Between Two Conflicts, Afghans Flee Iran |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/23/world/asia/iran-afghanistan-border-refugees.html |work=[[The New York Times]] |date=23 March 2026}}</ref> Arafat Jamal, the UNHCR Representative in Afghanistan, noted that they faced limited options amid the [[2026 Afghanistan–Pakistan War|military confrontations between Afghanistan and Pakistan]].<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |title=UN: Thousands of Afghan Migrants Return Daily Amid Rising Conflict in Iran |url=https://thekabultribune.com/en/0006638 |work=The Kabul Tribune |date=10 March 2026}}</ref>

According to ''[[The Guardian]]'', the war has overshadowed the executions of political prisoners in Iran, including those involved in the mass protests of January 2026, allowing Iran to continue carrying out death sentences and other severe punishments with little international scrutiny.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |last=Johnson |first=Sarah |last2=Foumani |first2=Maryam |date=2 April 2026 |title='Everyone Is Thinking About Oil Prices': Is Iran Using the War to Hide a Surge in Executions? |url=https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/2026/apr/02/iran-iranian-protesters-surge-executions-death-penalty-war |access-date=2 April 2026 |work=[[The Guardian]] |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref> According to several Iran-focused human rights groups, the rate of sentencing and executions by hanging surged amidst the war. The groups state that in most of these cases, executed prisoners did not receive due process.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Mahoozi |first1=Sanam |last2=Solomon |first2=Erika |title=Executions Surge in Iran Since Cease-Fire, Rights Groups Say |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/05/15/world/middleeast/execution-iran-ceasefire.html |work=[[The New York Times]] |access-date=17 May 2026 |date=15 May 2026}}</ref>

==== Military ====
On 26 March, it was reported that children as young as 12 years of age may join Iran's war support, leading to concerns of the use of [[child soldiers]].<ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |url=https://www.iranintl.com/en/202603265637 |title=Children as Young as 12 Can Join War Support, IRGC Says |date=26 March 2026 |website=[[Iran International]]}}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |url=https://www.rferl.org/amp/33718169.html |title=Iran Recruits 12-Year-Olds As 'Combatants Defending The Homeland' |date=27 March 2026 |work=[[Radio Free Europe/Radio Liberty]]}}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |url=https://iran-hrm.com/2026/03/28/iran-uses-12-year-old-children-in-security-patrols/ |title=Iran Uses 12-Year-Old Children in Security Patrols |website=Iran HRM |date=28 March 2026}}</ref> Days later, rights groups stated an 11-year-old was killed while on duty at a Tehran checkpoint.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |url=https://www.iranintl.com/en/202603297166 |title=Rights Group Says 11-Year-Old Killed 'While on Duty' at Tehran Checkpoint |date=29 March 2026 |website=[[Iran International]]}}</ref>

On 29 March, footage showed Iraq's [[Popular Mobilization Forces]] deployed in Iran.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |url=https://www.iranintl.com/en/202603299955 |title=Video: Iraq's Popular Mobilization Forces in Abadan |date=29 March 2026 |website=[[Iran International]]}}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |url=https://www.iranintl.com/en/202603299203 |title=Iraqi Forces Stationed in IRGC Housing in Bandar Abbas |date=29 March 2026 |website=[[Iran International]]}}</ref><ref name="iranintl28march"/>

A leaked US intelligence report concluded in May that Iran retained 70% of missile and launchers relative to pre-war status.<ref name="theguardian17july"/>

==== Cultural heritage sites ====
{{Main|Destruction of cultural heritage sites during the 2026 Iran War}}

[[File:Chehel Sotoon.jpg|thumb|[[Chehel Sotoun]] palace and its rich ''[[ayeneh-kari]]'' decorations was assessed to be the most affected.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Babaie |first1=Sussan |title=In a Month of War, Iran's Cultural Heritage Has Suffered Huge Damage |url=https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2026/04/01/in-just-a-few-weeks-irans-cultural-heritage-hassuffered-huge-damage |work=The  pArt Newspaper - International art pnews and events |date=1 April 2026 |language=en}}</ref>]]
Multiple historic and cultural sites, including [[UNESCO World Heritage Sites]], were damaged. The most affected was [[Chehel Sotoun]] and its rich ''[[ayeneh-kari]]''.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Babaie |first1=Sussan |title=In a Month of War, Iran's Cultural Heritage Has Suffered Huge Damage |url=https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2026/04/01/in-just-a-few-weeks-irans-cultural-heritage-has-suffered-huge-damage |work=The Art Newspaper - International art news and events |date=1 April 2026 |language=en}}</ref> On 2 March, a strike on [[Arg Square]] damaged nearby [[Golestan Palace]], prompting [[UNESCO]] to issue a statement of concern.<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Charles |first=Starr |date=5 March 2026 |title=US-Israeli Airstrike Damages UNESCO-Listed Palace in Tehran |url=https://www.dezeen.com/2026/03/05/golestan-palace-damaged-unesco-world-heritage/ |access-date=10 March 2026 |magazine=[[Dezeen]]}}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |last=Geranpayeh |first=Sarvy |date=3 March 2026 |title=Tehran's Unesco-Listed Golestan Palace Reportedly Damaged by US-Israeli Strikes |url=https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2026/03/03/us-israeli-strikes-damage-unesco-listed-golestan-palace-tehran |access-date=10 March 2026 |website=The Art Newspaper |language=en}}</ref> On 5 March, the [[Azadi Sport Complex]] was bombed.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |last=Zirin |first=Dave |author-link=Dave Zirin |date=11 March 2026 |title=The Bombing of Iran's Azadi Stadium Is Straight Out of Israel's Gaza Script |url=https://www.thenation.com/article/society/azadi-stadium-iran-us-israel-destruction/ |access-date=12 March 2026 |language=en-US |issn=0027-8378 |work=The Nation}}</ref> An 8 March strike on [[Falak-ol-Aflak]], which was marked with a [[Hague Convention for the Protection of Cultural Property in the Event of Armed Conflict|blue shield emblem]], damaged several sections of the site.<ref name = "ArtNewspaper310"/> Strikes on [[Isfahan]] on 9 March damaged [[Naqsh-e Jahan Square]], Chehel Sotoun, [[Ali Qapu]], the [[Shah Mosque (Isfahan)|Shah Mosque]], [[Jameh Mosque of Isfahan|Jameh Mosque]], and [[Natural History Museum of Isfahan|Teymouri Hall]].<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |last=Fassihi |first=Farnaz |date=11 March 2026 |title=World Heritage Sites Hit in Airstrikes on Iran |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/11/world/middleeast/iran-heritage-sites-damaged.html |access-date=12 March 2026 |work=[[The New York Times]] |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref><ref name = "ArtNewspaper310">{{#invoke:cite |web |last=Geranpayeh |first=Sarvy |date=10 March 2026 |title=Unesco Sites in Iranian City of Isfahan Damaged by US-Israel Strikes |url=https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2026/03/10/unesco-sites-in-iranian-city-of-isfahan-and-others-across-countrydamaged-by-us-israel-strikes |access-date=10 March 2026 |website=The Art Newspaper |language=en}}</ref>

On 11 March, UNESCO urged protection for Iran's [[heritage sites]] and World Heritage Sites that have been damaged or are under high risk due to the war, alongside other [[historic site]]s in Israel, Lebanon, and the rest of West Asia.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |date=11 March 2026 |title=UNESCO Raises Concern over Damage to Iran's Heritage Sites Amid War, Urges Protection |url=https://www.trtworld.com/article/68e9ee629824 |access-date=12 March 2026 |work=[[TRT World]] |language=en}}</ref> On 17 March, blast waves and shrapnel damaged multiple palaces at [[Sa'dabad Complex]] in northern Tehran.<ref>{{cite web |title=Targerting Iran's Historical Sites Reflects Fear of Its Proud History |website=WANA |date=22 March 2026 |url=https://wanaen.com/targeting-irans-historical-sites-reflects-fear-of-its-proud-history/}}</ref> [[Russia|Russia's]] [[Ministry of Foreign Affairs (Russia)|Ministry of Foreign Affairs]] reported that strikes in Tehran on 1 April damaged the [[St. Nicholas Church, Tehran|St. Nicholas Orthodox Church]] and its auxiliary buildings.<ref>{{Cite press release |title=Comment by Foreign Ministry Spokeswoman Maria Zakharova Regarding the Damage of the St Nicholas Church in Tehran in a Missile Strike |date=3 April 2026 |publisher=[[Ministry of Foreign Affairs of the Russian Federation]] |url=https://mid.ru/en/foreign_policy/news/2090945/ |access-date=18 April 2026}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=3 April 2026 |title=Russian Orthodox Church Suffers 'Serious Damage' in US-Israeli Strikes on Iran — What We Know |url=https://www.palestinechronicle.com/russian-orthodox-church-suffers-serious-damage-in-us-israeli-strikes-on-iran-what-we-know/ |access-date=6 April 2026 |website=The Palestine Chronicle}}</ref> On 7 April, [[Destruction of the Rafi'-Nia synagogue|the Rafi'-Nia synagogue was fully destroyed]] by the IAF.<ref name="Ynet070426"/>

=== Israel ===
Israel declared a nationwide [[state of emergency]], saying that the strikes on Iran were the largest that it had ever launched.<ref name="Liebermann-2026"/><ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |date=1 March 2026 |title=Israeli Army Says Air Raids on Iran Largest Ever in Air Forces History |url=https://www.lbcgroup.tv/news/middleeastnews/908321/lbci-lebanon-articles/en |access-date=28 February 2026 |website=LBCIV7 |language=en}}</ref> Israeli schools and workspaces were closed and public gatherings were canceled.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |last1=Boxerman |first1=Aaron |last2=Pager |first2=Tyler |last3=Fassihi |first3=Farnaz |last4=Bergman |first4=Ronen |date=28 February 2026 |title=U.S.-Led Strike on Iran: Live Updates as Trump Calls for Government Overthrow |url=https://www.nytimes.com/live/2026/02/28/world/iran-strikes-trump |access-date=28 February 2026 |work=[[The New York Times]] |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331 |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/LDjEo |archive-date=1 March 2026}}</ref>

In the 40 days from the start of the war through the [[2026 Iran war ceasefire|8 April ceasefire]], there were a total of 650 missile attacks from Iran to Israel, the majority of which carried cluster munitions. About 16 of the missiles that made it past Israeli missile defenses carried conventional warheads, while 50 had cluster munitions. The attacks killed a total of 24 people, all of them civilians, and resulted in more than 7,000 injuries. Of the 24 civilians killed, 14 were struck by conventional explosive warheads and 10 by cluster submunitions. Two of the deaths were inside a sheltered space, with the other fatalities being people in unprotected areas.<ref>{{cite news |last=Fabian |first=Emanuel |url=https://www.timesofisrael.com/650-iranian-missiles-fired-24-killed-and-10800-israeli-strikes-the-war-in-numbers/ |title=The War in Numbers: 650 Iranian Missiles Fired; 24 Killed in Israel, West Bank; 10,800 Israeli Strikes |work=[[The Times of Israel]] |date=10 April 2026 |access-date=29 April 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260410034107/https://www.timesofisrael.com/650-iranian-missiles-fired-24-killed-and-10800-israeli-strikes-the-war-in-numbers/ |archive-date=10 April 2026 |quote=With a ceasefire in Iran taking effect early Wednesday morning, the full scope of the Israeli-American 40-day campaign against the Islamic Republic began to emerge.... Iran, during the fighting, launched some 650 ballistic missiles at Israel. According to the military, more than half of the missiles carried cluster bomb warheads, which spread bomblets indiscriminately over wide areas. The missile attacks killed 20 Israeli civilians and foreign nationals in Israel, along with four Palestinians in the West Bank.... In all, at least 16 missiles carrying conventional warheads — with hundreds of kilograms of explosives — struck populated areas in Israel, causing extensive damage. There were also some 50 incidents of missiles carrying cluster bomb warheads hitting populated areas, with hundreds of separate impact sites.... All 24 fatalities from Iran's missile attacks were civilians, and all but two were not inside bomb shelters. Cluster munitions killed 10, and conventional missiles killed 14.}}</ref>

=== Palestine ===
{{See also|Gaza war}}

The Israeli [[Coordinator of Government Activities in the Territories]] closed multiple aid crossings in the [[Gaza Strip]], specifically interrupting free circulation on the [[Egypt–Gaza border]].<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |date=1 March 2026 |title=Israel Closes Gaza's Rafah Crossing Amid Attacks on Iran |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/3/1/israel-closes-gazas-rafah-crossing-amid-attacks-on-iran |work=[[Al Jazeera English]] |access-date=1 March 2026}}</ref> Israeli forces suspended [[United Nations]] humanitarian movements and postponed planned rotations of international humanitarian staff, including medical evacuations and the return of people into Gaza, which the [[United Nations Office for the Coordination of Humanitarian Affairs]] condemned.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |date=2 March 2026 |title=Today's Top News: Middle East, Occupied Palestinian Territory, Sudan |url=https://www.unocha.org/news/todays-top-news-middle-east-occupied-palestinian-territory-sudan |access-date=3 March 2026 |website=[[United Nations Office for the Coordination of Humanitarian Affairs]] |language=en}}</ref>

[[Israeli settler violence]] against [[Palestinians]] in [[occupied West Bank]] has increased; settlers have taken advantage of curbs on movement imposed during the renewed [[Israeli state of emergency]]. The renewed blocking of West Bank roads in the context of the [[2026 Lebanon war]] has delayed medics from assisting injured Palestinians.<ref>{{Cite news |date=11 March 2026 |title=Israeli Settler Violence Rises in West Bank Under Iran War Curbs |url=https://english.aawsat.com/node/5250010 |work=[[Asharq Al-Awsat]]}}</ref> The EU stated on 10 March that the Israeli actions were unacceptable and urged the Israeli authorities to make immediate efforts to prevent the violence against Palestinian civilians under [[international law]], warning that "impunity for such acts risks provoking further violence".<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |date=11 March 2026 |title=EU Urges Israel to Stop 'Unacceptable' Violence by Illegal Settlers in Occupied West Bank |url=https://www.trtworld.com/article/90d81b0c5221 |access-date=12 March 2026 |work=[[TRT World]] |language=en}}</ref>

=== Lebanese displacements ===

Lebanon stated in March that over one million people,<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |date=11 March 2026 |title=UN Warns of Widening Crisis as Israeli Attacks Displace 816,000 In Lebanon |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/3/11/un-warns-of-widening-crisis-as-israeli-attacks-displace-750000-in-lebanon |access-date=13 March 2026 |work=[[Al Jazeera English]] |language=en}}</ref> 1/6th of the population, had been displaced.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |date=8 March 2026 |title=Live Updates: Iran's President Says Nation 'Will Not Bow' to Pressure from US and Israel |url=https://apnews.com/live/iran-war-israel-trump-03-08-2026 |access-date=8 March 2026 |work=[[Associated Press]] |language=en}}</ref>

=== Gulf Arab states ===
The [[Arab states of the Persian Gulf]] started a campaign of prosecution against civilians and foreign citizens that shared or reposted rumours from unverified sources about the Iranian attacks, with the stated main goal to curb misinformation that could harm public order.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |date=2 March 2026 |title=UAE Warns People Against Sharing Unverified Information on Iranian Strikes |url=https://www.newarab.com/news/uae-warns-prosecution-misinformation-iranian-attacks |access-date=3 March 2026 |work=[[The New Arab]]}}</ref> Bahrain's Cyber Crime Directorate also started a campaign of arresting civilians in the country, which has a large [[Shia in Bahrain|Shiite minority]], who allegedly expressed "their support for the Iranian shelling targeting the Kingdom of Bahrain", describing such activities as treason and as promotion of hostile acts against the state.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |date=2 March 2026 |title=Bahrain Cracks Down on People Allegedly Celebrating Iran Strikes on Kingdom |url=https://www.newarab.com/news/bahrain-detains-people-allegedly-celebrating-iran-strikes |access-date=3 March 2026 |work=[[The New Arab]]}}</ref> Trump said "Even if we knew Gulf countries would be hit, big deal we did what we have to do".<ref name="UrasEtAl">{{#invoke:cite |news |last1=Uras |first1=Umut |first2=Sarah |last2=Haider |first3=Mariamne |last3=Everett |first4=Elis |last4=Gjevori |date=16 March 2026 |title=Trump Rips Allies for Lack of 'Enthusiasm'; Attack Sets UAE Oilfield Ablaze |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/liveblog/2026/3/16/iran-war-live-tehran-rejects-trump-claim-on-talks-gulf-attacks-continue |access-date=16 March 2026 |work=[[Al Jazeera English]] |language=en}}</ref>

Saudi Arabia invoked its [[Strategic Mutual Defence Agreement]] with Pakistan. Pakistan deployed some 8,000 troops, a squadron of 16 aircraft, and two squadrons of drones. There was a pledge to send more if required.<ref>{{Cite news |date=18 May 2026 |title=Exclusive: Pakistan Deploys Jet Squadron, Thousands of Troops to Saudi Arabia During Iran War |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/asia-pacific/pakistan-deploys-jet-squadron-thousands-troops-saudi-arabia-during-iran-war-2026-05-18/ |work=[[Reuters]]}}</ref>

On 28 May, the US president [[Donald Trump]] threatened to "blow up" Oman if it failed to "behave" in a casual aside during a cabinet meeting after reports of talks between Iran and Oman about jointly charging a toll for ships passing through the [[Strait of Hormuz]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Campbell |first=Lucy |date=28 May 2026 |title=Trump Threatens to 'Blow Up' US Ally Oman Amid Talks over Strait of Hormuz |url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/may/27/donald-trump-oman-threat-strait-hormuz |access-date=28 May 2026 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref>

== Economic impacts ==
{{Main|Economic impact of the 2026 Iran war|2026 Iran war regional mobilizations|Evacuations during the 2026 Iran war}}

[[File:20260309 DM MiddleEast Repatriations(1).pdf|thumb|A map of emergency evacuation of persons to Europe]]

<!-- This section should be very brief, given it has an article of its own. Expansion of this article should be carefully considered per [[WP:ARTICLESIZE]] -->
The war had global economic consequences: [[Price of oil|oil]] and [[Natural gas prices|gas prices]] surged, aviation and tourism were disrupted, and financial markets shuddered.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |url=https://www.oxfordeconomics.com/resource/the-2026-iran-war-an-initial-take-and-implications |title=The 2026 Iran War, An Initial Take and Implications |website=Oxford Economics |date=2 March 2026 |access-date=2 March 2026}}{{Dead link|date=March 2026 |bot=InternetArchiveBot }}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/middle-east/iran-conflict-poses-new-risk-us-economic-resilience-2026-03-02 |title=Iran Conflict Poses New Risk to US Economic Resilience |work=[[Reuters]] |date=2 March 2026 |access-date=2 March 2026}}</ref> [[International Energy Agency]] (IEA) head [[Fatih Birol]] described the situation as the "greatest global energy security challenge in history".<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |url=https://www.middleeasteye.net/live-blog/live-blog-update/iea-chief-world-faces-greatest-global-energy-security-challenge-history |title=IEA Chief: World Faces 'Greatest Global Energy Security Challenge in History' |work=[[Middle East Eye]] |date=20 March 2026 |access-date=21 March 2026}}</ref>

The Iranian regime's spokesperson, Fatemeh Mohajerani, estimated the direct and indirect cost of 40 days of war by US$270bn, or 90% of the estimated Iranian GDP in 2026. The FDD that cites Mohajerani, estimates the damage even up to $300bn with a middle estimation range of $144bn.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Swift |first=Daniel |last2=Maleki |first2=Miad |date=26 June 2026 |title=Iran's economic catastrophe |url=https://www.fdd.org/analysis/2026/06/26/irans-economic-catastrophe/ |access-date=25 July 2026 |website=FDD |language=en}}</ref>

According to [[Center for American Progress|CAP]], as of 14 July, Americans had paid more than $68 billion in extra costs for gas and diesel since the beginning of the war, in addition to the direct war costs.<ref>{{Cite web |date=15 July 2026 |title=Fact Sheet: Costs of the Trump Administration's War in Iran |url=https://www.americanprogress.org/article/fact-sheet-costs-of-the-trump-administrations-war-in-iran/ |access-date=25 July 2026 |website=Center for American Progress |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Iran War Energy Cost Tracker |url=https://iranwarcost.watson.brown.edu/ |access-date=25 July 2026 |website=iranwarcost.watson.brown.edu}}</ref>

=== Energy ===
[[File:2020- Price of oil.svg|thumb|The price of oil has been affected internationally at the times of the [[COVID-19 pandemic]], the [[Russo-Ukrainian war (2022–present)|2022- Russo-Ukrainian war]] and the 2026 Iran war.<ref name=EIA_OilPriceData>{{cite web |title=Petroleum & Other Liquids / Data ▼ / Download Series History |url=https://www.eia.gov/dnav/pet/pet_pri_spt_s1_w.htm |publisher=U.S. Energy Information Administration (EIA) |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260605153644/https://www.eia.gov/dnav/pet/pet_pri_spt_s1_w.htm |archive-date=5 June 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>]]
[[File:2025- Consumer Price Index - monthly.svg|thumb|The US [[Consumer price index in the United States|consumer price index]] (CPI) increased at the outbreak of the Iran war.<ref name="CNN_CPI_20251211">{{cite news |last1=Dale |first1=Daniel |date=11 December 2025 |title=Analysis: How the White House Is Using Misleading Comparisons to Make Inflation Sound Better |url=https://www.cnn.com/2025/12/11/politics/inflation-trump-karoline-leavitt-prices |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251217063806/https://www.cnn.com/2025/12/11/politics/inflation-trump-karoline-leavitt-prices |archive-date=17 December 2025 |agency=CNN}} ●{{nbsp}}Data from BLS: {{cite web |title=12-month percentage change, Consumer Price Index, selected categories |url=https://www.bls.gov/charts/consumer-price-index/consumer-price-index-by-category-line-chart.htm |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251217065215/https://www.bls.gov/charts/consumer-price-index/consumer-price-index-by-category-line-chart.htm |archive-date=17 December 2025 |publisher=US Bureau of Labor Statistics}} ●{{nbsp}}Higher-precision data from Federal Reserve: {{cite web |title=Consumer Price Index for All Urban Consumers: All Items in U.S. City Average (CPIAUCSL) |url=https://fred.stlouisfed.org/graph/?g=1LiHz |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251217070037/https://fred.stlouisfed.org/graph/?g=1LiHz |archive-date=17 December 2025 |publisher=US Federal Reserve, St. Louis office}}</ref>]]
[[File:2021- Retail price of gasoline (US).svg|thumb|The retail price of gasoline in the US increased at the outbreak of wars in Ukraine and Iran.<ref name=EIAgasPrices>Data from {{cite web |title=Petroleum & Other Liquids / Data / Weekly U.S. All Grades All Formulations Retail Gasoline Prices |url=https://www.eia.gov/dnav/pet/hist/LeafHandler.ashx?n=pet&s=emm_epm0_pte_nus_dpg&f=w |website=U.S. Energy Information Administration |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260508185722/https://www.eia.gov/dnav/pet/hist/LeafHandler.ashx?n=pet&s=emm_epm0_pte_nus_dpg&f=w |archive-date=8 May 2026 |date=2026 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name=NYTimes_20260420>{{cite news |last1=DePillis |first1=Lydia |title=Why Gas Prices Go Up Fast and Take So Long to Fall |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/20/business/economy/gas-stations-pricing.html |newspaper=The New York Times |date=20 April 2026 |quote=Source: AAA for national average unleaded retail gasoline prices.}}</ref>]]
Oil and natural gas prices rose in direct response to Iranian threats to attack tankers and other ships and to Iranian strikes on oil facilities in Gulf producing countries.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/mar/02/middle-east-crisis-oil-prices-inflation-us-iran-interest-rates-growth |title=Middle East Crisis Pushes up Oil Prices – and Could Drive Inflation Rises Too |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=2 March 2026 |access-date=2 March 2026}}</ref> 20% of global oil and gas production normally transit the Strait of Hormuz.<ref name="Buchanan-2026" /> As the war continued, oil and gas prices ebbed and flowed as markets attempted to assess the likelihood of an end to the conflict. Prices ranged from below $70 before the war<ref>{{Cite web |title=Brent Crude Oil Prices (1987-2026) |url=https://www.macrotrends.net/2480/brent-crude-oil-prices-10-year-daily-chart |access-date=16 April 2026 |website=Macrotrends}}</ref> to as high as $139 on 7 March.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Zhang |first1=Qi |last2=Hu |first2=Yi |last3=Jiao |first3=Jianbin |last4=Wang |first4=Shouyang |date=2 January 2024 |title=The Impact of Russia–Ukraine War on Crude Oil Prices: An EMC Framework |journal=Humanities and Social Sciences Communications |language=en |volume=11 |issue=1 |pages=8 |doi=10.1057/s41599-023-02526-9 |issn=2662-9992 |doi-access=free}}</ref> Gas prices also rose.<ref name="bloomberg4march" /> Following the start of the war, multiple oil producers such as [[Shell plc|Shell]], [[TotalEnergies]], [[BP|Britain's BP]] and others announced increased profits in their first quarter.<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Schmidt |first1=Gregory |last2=Elliott |first2=Rebecca F. |date=7 May 2026 |title=Shell Reports Nearly $7 Billion Profit After Oil Prices Surged Amid U.S.-Iran War |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/05/07/business/shell-profit-oil-iran-war.html |access-date=7 May 2026 |work=[[The New York Times]] |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref>

Toward the end of March significant shortages of domestic [[petroleum product]]s emerged in [[southeast Asia]] as reserves ran out.<ref name=bbc-20260324>{{#invoke:cite |news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c1450zj6n48o |title=Everyday Life in Asia Is Being Upended by Iran War Fuel Crisis |last1=Ewe |first1=ByKoh |last2=Drury |first2=Flora |work=[[BBC News]] |date=24 March 2026 |access-date=25 March 2026}}</ref>

By July 2026, [[Brent Crude|brent]] prices fell from a pick of $118.35 in 31 March 2026 to $71.57 in 1 July 2026.<ref>{{Cite web |date=22 July 2026 |title=Crude Oil Prices: Brent - Europe |url=https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/DCOILBRENTEU |access-date=25 July 2026 |website=fred.stlouisfed.org |language=en}}</ref> As tension in the gulf started to increase the brent price increased to $100.69 on 23 July, but on 24 July, the brent price closed at $96.78<ref>{{Cite web |title=Oil Price Charts |url=https://oilprice.com/oil-price-charts/ |access-date=25 July 2026 |website=OilPrice.com |language=en}}</ref>, and oil shipping index which picked to 3,737 in March 2026 has declined to 1,850 in July 2026.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Baltic Dirty Tanker Index Stock Performance Report |url=https://www.barchart.com/stocks/quotes/BDTI.BI/performance |access-date=24 July 2026 |website=Barchart.com |language=en}}</ref>

[[File:USS Bainbridge (DDG 96) transits the Suez Canal during Operation Epic Fury (9552415).jpg|thumb|The guided-missile destroyer {{USS|Bainbridge|DDG-96|6}} passes through the [[Suez Canal]] during Operation Epic Fury, March 2026]]
Stock markets initially fell, with the [[Dow Jones Industrial Average]] falling over 400 points (one percent) on 2 March alone,<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |url=https://www.npr.org/2026/03/02/nx-s1-5732287/iran-war-oil-gasoline-prices |title=Oil Prices Surge and Stocks Fall Amid Fears over Iran War |work=[[NPR]] |date=2 March 2026 |access-date=2 March 2026 |archive-date=2 March 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260302152122/https://www.npr.org/2026/03/02/nx-s1-5732287/iran-war-oil-gasoline-prices |url-status=live}}</ref> although by mid-April had recovered and reached record highs.<ref>{{Cite news |title=Stock Market Data – US Markets, World Markets, And Stock Quotes |url=https://www.cnn.com/markets |access-date=16 April 2026 |work=[[CNN]] |language=en}}</ref> By 24 July 2026 the market gained more than 17% from the bottom in 31 March 2026, closing at 7,411.98.<ref>{{Cite web |date=24 July 2026 |title=S&P 500 |url=https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/SP500 |access-date=25 July 2026 |website=fred.stlouisfed.org |language=en}}</ref>

The UN [[World Food Programme]] and various economic analysts warned that the war is driving significant, long-term increases in global [[food prices]].<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |last1=Del Valle |first1=Magdalena |title=Hormuz Disruptions Will Hit Food Prices as Well as Oil, UN Warns |url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-03-10/hormuz-disruptions-in-iran-war-will-hit-food-prices-not-just-oil-un |website=[[Bloomberg News]] |date=10 March 2026}}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |title=WFP Warns Rising Food and Fuel Prices Risk Pushing Global Hunger Higher as Humanitarian Needs Grow |url=https://www.wfp.org/news/wfp-warns-rising-food-and-fuel-prices-risk-pushing-global-hunger-higher-humanitarian-needs |website=[[World Food Programme]] |date=8 March 2026}}</ref>

An analysis performed by ''[[The New York Times]]'', comparing the quantities and value of oil and other fuels shipped between the start of the war and 8 May 2026, and those from the same period a year earlier showed that the biggest beneficiaries of the market changes were the United States (which saw an increase in exports and a revenue increase of about $50 billion) and Russia (which had steady exports and an increase of more than $15 billion in revenue), while other major non-Persian Gulf exporters also benefitted to lesser degrees. All Persian Gulf nations showed declines in exports, though those that were able to avoid shipping bottlenecks through the Strait of Hormuz (Saudi Arabia through pipelines to the Red Sea, and Oman via geography) saw increases in revenue, while those that could not avoid the strait (Iraq, Kuwait, Qatar and the United Arab Emirates) saw declines in revenue. Iran, which had access to the strait, had an increase in revenues.<ref>Fan, Amy; Elliott, Rebecca F. [https://www.nytimes.com/2026/05/16/business/energy-environment/iran-war-oil-countries-winners-losers.html "The Countries Profiting From the War Oil Shock, as Others Lose Out"], ''[[The New York Times]]'', 16 May 20206, updated 22 May 2026 Accessed 24 May 2026. "But inside the Persian Gulf, the story is much more complicated. The effective closing of the Strait of Hormuz, a choke point between the Gulf and the rest of the world, has forced the United Arab Emirates, Iraq and other countries to slash production and exports. Some are hurting worse than others. Those that can use pipelines to reroute their oil to ports away from the strait have fared a lot better than countries without such options."</ref>

=== Commodities ===
Disruption in the supply of essential [[fertilizer]]s due to the closure of the Strait threatened food production in many countries.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |title=Farmers See Fertiliser Price Surge as Iran War Blocks Exports, Threatening Losses |url=https://www.reuters.com/business/energy/iran-war-threatens-asia-fertiliser-supplies-ahead-planting-season-2026-03-05/ |work=[[Reuters]] |date=5 March 2026}}</ref> The Fertilizer Institute stated that nearly 50% of global [[urea]] and [[sulfur]] exports annually transited through the Strait of Hormuz, made worse by the natural gas shortage, which is a key feedstock for [[nitrogen fertilizer]]s.<ref name="Buchanan-2026">{{#invoke:cite |news |last1=Buchanan |first1=Naomi |title=There's a Major Inflation Risk Lurking for the Economy as the Iran War Drags On, And It's Not Oil |url=https://www.businessinsider.com/food-inflation-oil-prices-fertilizer-farmers-iran-war-shipping-hormuz-2026-3 |work=[[Business Insider]] |date=11 March 2026}}</ref>

=== Air travel ===
[[File:Grounded airliners at Manchester Airport Feb 2026.jpg|thumb|Airliners of [[Qatar Airways]], [[Kuwait Airways]] and [[Emirates (airline)|Emirates]] grounded at [[Manchester Airport]], 28 February 2026.]]
Iran's airspace emptied of civilian aircraft during the conflict,<ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |date=28 February 2026 |title=Iran's Skies Empty After Strikes as Regional States Close Airspace |url=https://www.iranintl.com/en/202602284853 |access-date=28 February 2026 |website=[[Iran International]] |language=en}}</ref> as Bahrain, Iraq, Israel, Kuwait, Qatar, Syria and the UAE closed their respective airspaces.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |last=Yosef |first=Eugenia |date=28 February 2026 |title=Israel Closes Airspace After Strikes on Iran |url=https://www.cnn.com/world/live-news/israel-iran-attack-02-28-26-hnk-intl?post-id=cmm5yjlf6000v3b6parlqyd31 |access-date=28 February 2026 |work=[[CNN]] |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |title=Iraq Shuttering Airspace After US, Israel Strike Iran |url=https://www.timesofisrael.com/liveblog_entry/iraq-shuttering-airspace-after-us-israel-strike-iran/ |first=Noam |last=Lehmann |date=28 February 2026 |work=[[The Times of Israel]]}}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |date=28 February 2026 |title=Syria Shutters Airspace |url=https://www.timesofisrael.com/liveblog_entry/syria-shutters-airspace/ |access-date=28 February 2026 |work=[[The Times of Israel]] |language=en-US |issn=0040-7909}}</ref> Some carriers suspended service to the Middle East.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |first1=Frederico |last1=Maccioni |first2=Joanna |last2=Plucinska |title=Travel in Chaos as Airlines Cancel Flights After US, Israel Strikes on Iran |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/europe/airlines-suspend-middle-east-flights-after-us-israel-strikes-iran-2026-02-28/ |work=[[Reuters]] |date=28 February 2026}}</ref>

The disruption in global oil supply, caused by the closure of the Strait of Hormuz during the conflict, led to higher jet fuel prices, which increased operating costs for airlines and pushed up airfares on both domestic and international routes. Airlines responded by raising ticket prices and adjusting some flight schedules to cope with higher fuel costs. This impact was also visible in Europe, where continued disruptions in energy supply routes contributed to higher fuel prices and additional pressure on airlines. As a result, some carriers reduced capacity or cut flights, while others passed increased costs directly on to passengers.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Iacurci |first=Greg |date=2 May 2026 |title=Airfare Amid Iran War: Buy Now or Wait out the Conflict? Experts Weigh the Risks |url=https://www.cnbc.com/2026/05/02/iran-war-airfare-travel-airlines-plane-tickets-when-to-buy.html |access-date=7 May 2026 |website=CNBC |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2 May 2026 |title=How the Iran War Is Hurting Travelers, Airline Industry |url=https://www.dw.com/en/how-the-iran-war-is-hurting-travelers-airline-industry/a-77000742 |access-date=7 May 2026 |website=dw.com |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=29 April 2026 |title=Iran War, Jet Fuel Concerns Cloud Airlines' Summer Holiday Plans |url=https://www.reuters.com/business/iran-war-jet-fuel-concerns-cloud-airlines-summer-holiday-plans-2026-04-29/ |website=Reuters}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Rose |first=Joel |date=24 April 2026 |title=Airlines Face Headwinds as Iran War Leads to Rising Fuel Costs |url=https://www.npr.org/2026/04/24/nx-s1-5797455/airlines-face-headwinds-as-iran-war-leads-to-rising-fuel-costs |access-date=7 May 2026 |work=NPR |language=en}}</ref>

[[Spirit Airlines]], an American [[low-cost airline]], ceased operations on 2 May 2026, after increased fuel costs from the war exacerbated its existing financial difficulties.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Valle |first1=Sabrina |last2=Shepardson |first2=David |last3=Singh |first3=Rajesh Kumar |last4=Oladipo |first4=Doyinsola |title=Spirit Airlines Shuts Down, Industry's First Iran War Casualty |url=https://www.reuters.com/business/spirit-airlines-prepares-cease-operations-after-rescue-deal-stalls-wsj-reports-2026-05-01/ |access-date=2 May 2026 |work=[[Reuters]] |date=2 May 2026}}</ref>

