=== Strait of Bab-el-Mandeb ===
{{See also|Red Sea crisis}}

[[Houthi-controlled Yemen]] threatened shipping in the [[Gulf of Aden]] and [[Red Sea]]. Its threats had been suspended since 10 October 2025.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |date=28 February 2026 |title=Houthis Threaten to 'Escalate Attacks' on Red Sea-Linked Bab el-Mandeb Strait: 4 Ways This Could Hurt the Indian Economy |url=https://www.financialexpress.com/business/news/houthis-threaten-to-escalate-attacks-on-red-sea-linked-bab-el-mandeb-strait-4-ways-this-could-hurt-the-indian-economynbsp/4158147/ |access-date=3 March 2026 |website=[[The Financial Express (India)|The Financial Express]] |language=en}}</ref> March threats by [[Houthis|Houthi]] leader [[Abdul-Malik al-Houthi]] had not stopped Red Sea traffic as of mid-April, although traffic remained under historic levels after crashing in 2024 as a result of Houthi attacks.<ref>{{Cite news |date=28 March 2026 |title=Potential Houthi Threat to Red Sea Shipping Could Further Damage Global Economy |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cwyvd5z1xjno |access-date=16 April 2026 |work=[[BBC News]] |language=en-GB}}</ref> On 15 March, the ''[[Associated Press]]'' reported that the weapons stockpile of the Houthis was running low from repeated attacks on Israel, while Iran had not been able to resupply them, because of the war.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |last=Khaled |first=Fatma |date=15 March 2026 |title=As Other Iran-Allied Groups Are Engaging in the Mideast War, Yemen's Houthis Hold Back |url=https://apnews.com/article/yemen-houthis-iran-war-gulf-us-israel-1fc2a646d0cc42131385e7e61c409565 |access-date=19 March 2026 |work=[[Associated Press]]}}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |last=Khaled |first=Fatma |date=15 March 2026 |title=As Iran's Other Proxy Groups Join War, Houthi Rebels in Yemen Hold Back, For Now |url=https://www.timesofisrael.com/as-irans-other-proxy-groups-join-war-houthi-rebels-in-yemen-hold-back-for-now/ |access-date=19 March 2026 |work=[[The Times of Israel]]}}</ref>

=== Internet infrastructure ===
{{Further|Cyberwarfare during the 2026 Iran war}}

Three [[Amazon Web Services]] data centers in the United Arab Emirates were struck and damaged as a result of [[drone strikes]], leading to outages of web infrastructure within the Middle East,<ref>{{#invoke:cite |web |last=Gault |first=Matthew |date=2 March 2026 |title=Amazon Data Centers on Fire After Iranian Missile Strikes on Dubai |url=https://www.404media.co/amazon-data-centers-on-fire-after-iranian-missile-strikes-on-dubai/ |access-date=3 March 2026 |website=[[404 Media]] |language=en}}</ref> causing "sparks and fire" as well as "major structural damage" for 2 of the centers, with internal water levels reaching up to {{Val|4|u=cm}}, worsened by the failure of cooling and air systems. Fundamental web infrastructure including [[Amazon S3|S3 storage]], [[Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud|EC2 compute]] and [[Amazon DynamoDB|DynamoDB databases]] suffered complete outages in early March.<ref>{{#invoke:cite |news |first1=Eugene |last1=Kim |first2=Lee Chong |last2=Ming |date=3 March 2026 |title=Amazon Says 3 Data Centers Were Damaged by Drone Strikes in the Middle East |url=https://www.businessinsider.com/amazon-data-centers-middle-east-drone-strukes-us-iran-conflict-2026-3 |access-date=3 March 2026 |work=[[Business Insider]] |language=en-US}}</ref> Iran shut down Internet access for its people throughout the war.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Down |first=Aisha |date=6 April 2026 |title=Iran's Internet Blackout Is Longest National Shutdown Since Arab Spring |url=https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/apr/06/iran-internet-blackout-is-longest-national-shutdown-since-arab-spring |access-date=16 April 2026 |work=[[The Guardian]] |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref>

=== Military costs ===
[[File:Hearings to examine a review of the President's supplemental funding request of June 24, 2026 (July 21, 2026).webm|thumb|thumbtime=16:23|Defense Secretary [[Pete Hegseth]], Joint Chiefs of Staff Chair [[Dan Caine]], and Agriculture Secretary [[Brooke Rollins]] testifying before the [[Senate Appropriations Committee]] to testify on President Trump's $87.6 billion funding request for the war in July 2026]]
As of early April, US military costs were estimated at $25–35{{nbsp}}billion, according to the [[American Enterprise Institute]],<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Judah |first1=Jacob |last2=Mackinnon |first2=Amy |date=7 April 2026 |title='Hundreds of Millions of Dollars a Day': US Counts Cost of War on Iran |url=https://www.irishtimes.com/world/middle-east/2026/04/07/hundreds-of-millions-of-dollars-a-day-us-counts-equipment-cost-of-iran-war/ |access-date=16 April 2026 |newspaper=[[The Irish Times]] |language=en}}</ref> and Israeli costs at $11{{nbsp}}billion, according to [[Ministry of Finance (Israel)|Israel's Finance Ministry]].<ref>{{Cite news |date=12 April 2026 |title=Iran War Has Cost Israel $11.5{{nbsp}}billion in Budgetary Expenses, Ministry Says |url=https://english.alarabiya.net/News/middle-east/2026/04/12/iran-war-has-cost-israel-115-billion-in-budgetary-expenses-ministry-says |access-date=16 April 2026 |work=[[Al Arabiya English]] |language=en}}</ref> Costs to the Iranian economy were estimated at $40–50{{nbsp}}billion, around 10% of [[Gross domestic product|GDP]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Shearing |first=Neal |date=17 March 2026 |title=How Will the Iran War Affect the Global Economy? |url=https://www.chathamhouse.org/2026/03/how-will-iran-war-affect-global-economy |access-date=16 April 2026 |website=[[Chatham House]] |language=en}}</ref>

According to a report by the [[Center for International Policy]] in early May, two months into the war, the US had spent nearly $72 billion on the war ($1.2 billion per day on average). The figure was nearly three times the amount of the Pentagon's official estimate.<ref>{{cite news |date=7 May 2026 |title=Trump Abandons "Project Freedom" After Saudi Arabia and Kuwait Suspend Access to Bases and Airspace |url=https://www.democracynow.org/2026/5/7/headlines/trump_abandons_project_freedom_after_saudi_arabia_and_kuwait_suspend_access_to_bases_and_airspace |access-date=9 May 2026 |work=Democracy Now! |language=en}}</ref>

In July, US defense secretary Pete Hegseth told a Senate committee that the US had spent $37.5 billion on the war. He requested an additional $67 billion to continue the war.<ref>{{Cite web |date=21 July 2026 |title=Live updates: Hegseth says Iran war cost $37.5 billion so far as administration seeks more funding |url=https://edition.cnn.com/2026/07/21/world/live-news/iran-war-trump |access-date=21 July 2026 |website=CNN |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Guilfoil |first=Kyla |date=21 July 2026 |title=Pete Hegseth puts cost of Iran war at $37.5 billion as the Pentagon seeks more money from Congress |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/trump-administration/iran-war-cost-estimate-37-billion-pete-hegseth-senate-hearing-rcna588619 |access-date=21 July 2026 |website=NBC News |language=en}}</ref>

Iran reportedly spent less on weapons than the US and Israel did. For example, Iran could pay $50,000 for a [[Shahed drone]], while [[MIM-104 Patriot|PATRIOT]] and [[THAAD]] interceptors to neutralize the drone could cost $4{{nbsp}}million and $12{{nbsp}}million respectively.<ref name="ndtv10march" />

The war has also increased indirect military costs due to the rapid depletion of US munitions stockpiles. According to reports, the United States has used large quantities of high-cost interceptors such as Patriot and [[Terminal High Altitude Area Defense|THAAD]] missiles, further increasing replacement expenses and strain on defense budgets. The depletion of stockpiles has also affected US arms transfers and procurement decisions, including reports that Washington is reconsidering or delaying the transfer of Tomahawk cruise missiles to Germany and reviewing Germany's request for [[Typhon missile system|Typhon]] launchers due to limited availability.<ref name="nbcnews14may">{{Cite web |date=14 May 2026 |title=The Pentagon Has No New Munitions Contracts Amid Concerns About a Weapons Shortage |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/national-security/pentagon-no-new-munitions-contracts-concerns-weapons-shortage-rcna342451 |access-date=10 June 2026 |website=NBC News |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last1=Cohen |first1=Zachary |last2=Bertrand |first2=Natasha |date=21 April 2026 |title=US at Risk of Running out of Missiles If Another War Breaks out After Depleting Stockpile in Iran Operations {{!}} CNN Politics |url=https://www.cnn.com/2026/04/21/politics/us-military-missile-stockpile |access-date=10 June 2026 |website=CNN |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=13 May 2026 |title=Pentagon Says US Has Spent $29 Billion on Iran War so Far, Amid Concerns over Weapons Stockpiles |url=https://www.euronews.com/2026/05/13/pentagon-says-us-has-spent-29-billion-on-iran-war-so-far-amid-concerns-over-weapons-stockp |access-date=10 June 2026 |website=www.euronews.com}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=4 June 2026 |title=Pentagon Likely to Cancel Missile Deal with Germany over Fears of Russia |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2026/06/04/us-germany-tomahawks-missiles-cancel-00950284 |access-date=10 June 2026 |website=POLITICO |language=en}}</ref> Reports also indicate delays in missile deliveries to Japan and new procurement efforts, including an $842 million agreement for [[AGM-158 JASSM|JASSM-ER]] cruise missiles from Denmark. US officials have raised concerns that existing stockpiles may be insufficient for sustained operations in multiple theaters, including in the event of a future conflict in Asia. The Pentagon has also expanded efforts to increase production capacity through programs aimed at scaling lower-cost "affordable mass" munitions and involving non-traditional defense contractors.<ref>{{Cite web |date=6 June 2026 |title=U.S. State Department Approves Potential $842 Million Danish Purchase of AGM-158 JASSM-ER Cruise Missiles |url=https://defence-industry.eu/u-s-state-department-approves-potential-842-million-danish-purchase-of-agm-158-jassm-er-cruise-missiles/ |access-date=10 June 2026 |website=Defence Industry |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Radzius |first=Darius |date=13 May 2026 |title=Pentagon Rushes to Buy 10,000 Missiles as Iran Has Depleted Stockpiles |url=https://www.military.com/pentagon-rushes-to-buy-10000-cheap-missiles-due-to-iran-war |access-date=10 June 2026 |website=Military.com |language=en}}</ref><ref name="nbcnews14may" /> The US defence industry saw record stock surges during the war.<ref>{{Cite web |date=18 April 2026 |title=U.S. Military Industrial Complex: The Real Winner of the Iran War|url=https://www.indiatoday.in/world/story/us-military-industrial-complex-profits-amid-iran-conflict-defence-stocks-rise-2898300-2026-04-18 |access-date=22 June 2026 |website=[[India Today]] |language=en}}</ref>

== Environmental impacts ==
{{Main|Environmental impact of war}}
Airstrikes on industrial and fuel hubs have released a chronic toxic cocktail of benzene, heavy metals, and persistent 'forever chemicals' ([[PFAS]]), triggering 'black rain' in major cities and causing long-term health degradation for civilians.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Poynting |first1=Mark |last2=Murray |first2=Alex |last3=Kayleen |first3=Devlinand |last4=Metzler |first4=Barbara |date=10 March 2026 |title=Why Air Strikes on Tehran Oil Facilities Are Causing Black Rain |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cqxd1nv3re2o |access-date=7 April 2026 |website=BBC |language=en-GB}}</ref>

The high oil prices driven by wars in the Middle East, including Iran, created a surge in profits of oil companies, pushing them to increase production as opposed to targets set by the [[Paris climate agreement]]. The planned expansion is projected to exceed the [[International Energy Agency]]'s regular scenario - a 5.9% increase in oil and gas production in the 2020s, leading to a {{cvt|2.9|C-change}} global temperature increase.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Watts |first1=Jonathan |title=Fuel on the fire: why oil companies are profiting as the world gets dangerously hot |url=https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/jul/07/big-oil-companies-profiting-fossil-fuel-global-climate-change |access-date=12 July 2026 |work=The Guardian |date=7 July 2026}}</ref>

