== Diplomatic efforts ==
{{see also|Islamabad Talks|Islamabad Memorandum}}
{{excerpt|2025–2026 Iran–United States negotiations|paragraphs=1-2}}

===June 2026 Memorandum of Understanding===
After concluding that continued hostilities would risk economic calamity,<ref name="graunr">{{cite news |last1=Roth |first1=Andrew |title=Trump Signs Iran Peace Plan, Claiming Deal Averts 'Worldwide Depression' |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/jun/17/trump-us-iran-war-mou-deal |access-date=18 June 2026 |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=17 June 2026}}</ref> Donald Trump signed a [[Islamabad Memorandum|memorandum of understanding]] (MOU) on behalf of the United States during a ceremony at the [[Palace of Versailles]] on 17 June 2026.<ref name="graunr"/> The signing followed an earlier, digital signing of the MOU by the foreign ministers of the two principal belligerents.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Freiberg |first1=Nava |title=US, Iran Said Weighing Second Electronic Signing of MOU Today Rather Than In-Person Ceremony Friday |url=https://www.timesofisrael.com/liveblog_entry/us-iran-said-weighing-second-electronic-signing-of-mou-today-rather-than-in-person-ceremony-friday/ |access-date=18 June 2026 |work=[[Times of Israel]] |date=18 June 2026}}</ref> In the MOU, the United States and Iran agreed to suspend hostilities for 60 days and to establish a framework for a permanent peace under which the US would make major financial and political concessions to the Islamic Republic of Iran.<ref name="cnbcbao">{{cite news |last1=Bao |first1=Anniek |title=Trump Claims Iran Deal Is 'Unconditional Surrender,' Says His Power Has 'No Limits': Axios |url=https://www.cnbc.com/2026/06/19/trump-claims-iran-deal-is-unconditional-surrender-axios-.html |access-date=18 June 2026 |work=[[CNBC]] |date=18 June 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=MoU's $300bn Iran Reconstruction Fund Becomes US Political Flashpoint |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/6/18/mous-300bn-iran-reconstruction-fund-becomes-us-political-flashpoint |access-date=18 June 2026 |work=[[Al Jazeera English]] |date=18 June 2026}}</ref><ref name="graunr"/>

Trump characterized the MOU as representing the "unconditional surrender" of Iran.<ref name="cnbcbao"/> Iran declared the document as an enduring "record of US failure".<ref name="graunr"/> The ''[[Foreign Policy (magazine)|Foreign Policy]]'', and ''[[The Atlantic]]'' described the outcome of the war as a "defeat" for the United States and Israel.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Musgrave |first1=Paul |title=Iran Is a Bigger Defeat Than Vietnam |url=https://foreignpolicy.com/2026/06/16/iran-vietnam-strategy-defeat/ |access-date=18 June 2026 |work=[[Foreign Policy (magazine)|Foreign Policy]] |date=18 June 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Wood |first1=Graeme |title=Iran Has Humiliated Trump |url=https://www.theatlantic.com/ideas/2026/06/iran-trump-war-defeat-deal/687595/ |access-date=18 June 2026 |work=[[The Atlantic]] |date=18 June 2026}}</ref> ''[[The Independent]]'' said that America had lost the war, and called Iran's now-proven ability to close of the Strait of Hormuz a "weapon of mass economic destruction of unique power" and "far more useful option than any nuclear device."<ref>{{cite web |title=America Has Lost Its War with Iran |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/voices/editorials/america-trump-iran-ceasefire-agreement-war-hormuz-b2995971.html |work=The Independent |access-date=19 June 2026 |date=15 June 2026}}</ref> Jeremy Brown, international editor for the [[BBC]], remarked that "the US and Israeli air forces scored a series of tactical victories. But they were not enough to avoid a strategic defeat."<ref>{{cite news |last1=Bowen |first1=Jeremy |title=US-Iran Deal Raises Inescapable Question of What the War Was For |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/clyegr2mp8jo |access-date=18 June 2026 |work=[[BBC News]] |date=18 June 2026}}</ref> [[Robert Kagan]], a senior fellow at the [[Brookings Institution]], said the deal was "a total victory for Iran and a total defeat for the United States," citing major US concessions. He called the increased Iranian control of the Strait of Hormuz "a major blow to the United States' position in the region, and it's also a major blow to Israel's security".<ref>{{cite web |title=Has the U.S. Lost the War in Iran? |url=https://www.npr.org/2026/05/24/nx-s1-5830110/has-the-u-s-lost-the-war-in-iran |work=NPR |access-date=19 June 2026 |date=24 May 2026}}</ref> American diplomat [[Richard N. Haass|Richard Haas]] described the deal as "a massive victory for Iran," pointing to the failure of the United States to topple the Iranian government, Iran's retention of its drone and missile arsenal, deferral of issues regarding its nuclear program, continued ability to support [[Axis of Resistance|its network of non-state allies in the Middle East]], sanctions relief and future reconstruction fund, and a commitment from the United States not to interfere in Iran's internal affairs. He also said US-Israel relations were badly damaged, and that Israel's "freedom of action has been significantly curtailed."<ref>{{cite web |last1=Fadel |first1=Leila |title=Who Won and Who Lost? Assessing the Fallout of the Iran War |url=https://www.npr.org/2026/06/19/nx-s1-5861743/who-won-and-who-lost-assessing-the-fallout-of-the-iran-war |website=npr.org |publisher=NPR |access-date=19 June 2026 |date=19 June 2026}}</ref> [[Paul Musgrave]], an associate professor of government at [[Georgetown University in Qatar]], said the deal indicated that "the Iranians had greater strategic patience, greater strategic leverage and the ability to shape a deal that was always going to be more favorable to them relative to the Americans." [[Susan Rice]] described the MOU as "a jaw-dropping, horrific surrender document complete with hundreds of billions in reparations."<ref>{{cite web |first=Michael |last=Koziol |title=What Was It All For? The Winners and Losers of Trump's Deal with Iran |url=https://www.smh.com.au/world/middle-east/what-was-it-all-for-the-winners-and-losers-of-trump-s-deal-with-iran-20260618-p607ul.html |website=[[Sydney Morning Herald]] |access-date=19 June 2026 |date=20 June 2026}}</ref> Writing for ''[[The Indian Express]]'', journalist Mashkoora Khan said that the outcome depended on who is being asked: for Trump, a decisive victory; for Iran, a defeat of Washington because it survived, adding that "Independent analysts are similarly divided, and many conclude that nearly everyone lost something."<ref>{{Cite web |date=15 June 2026 |title=Who Won the Iran-US War — Trump or Tehran? And Who Lost? |url=https://indianexpress.com/article/world/who-won-iran-war-trump-tehran-pakistan-munir-netanyahu-100-days-analysis-10738761/ |access-date=18 June 2026 |website=The Indian Express |language=en}}</ref>

Iranian chief negotiator Mohammad Bagher Ghalibaf stated Iran would unilaterally begin imposing permanent tolls on ships passing the Strait of Hormuz 60 days after the withdrawal of the US Navy.<ref name="graunr"/> The war helped entrench the power of the IRGC within Iranian politics and society,<ref>{{cite news |title=Gulf States Hesitant to Fund $300b. Iran Reconstruction, Worried of Reinforcing Regional Rival |url=https://www.jpost.com/middle-east/article-899829 |access-date=18 June 2026 |work=[[Jerusalem Post]] |date=18 June 2026}}</ref> and, according to [[Robert Pape]], began the process of transforming Iran into a major power.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Pape |first1=Robert |title=The War Is Turning Iran Into a Major World Power |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/06/opinion/iran-war-strait-hormuz.html |work=[[The New York Times]] |access-date=18 June 2026 |date=6 April 2026}}</ref>

== Analysis ==
{{Excerpt|Analysis of the 2026 Iran war|paragraphs=2,3}}
Donald Trump's threats to destroy Iranian infrastructure have drawn warnings from international law experts that such actions could constitute war crimes under the [[Rome Statute]],<ref>{{cite news |last1=Bateman |first1=Tom |title=Iran war shows norms of international conflicts have been overturned |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c4gjv02g54ko |agency=BBC News |date=24 March 2026}}</ref> and be actionable under [[universal jurisdiction]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Trump gloats on possible war crimes in Iran, but punishment distant |url=https://www.france24.com/en/live-news/20260402-trump-gloats-on-possible-war-crimes-in-iran-but-punishment-distant |agency=[[France 24]] |date=2 April 2026 |language=en}}</ref>

